{"parentUuid":"b03e2b56-215c-4837-8675-c5628d007a2b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ef194c20-d5f8-4d01-bce0-254fc908bea5","agentId":"aa8a0297ea9df7045","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XKgoAy9HtG4g7rQwLYtu3e","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Fixes applied: Supertraining Chapter 7 lead-in and 7.1 (Ch7a), 10 entries\n2\t\n3\tFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch7a_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES: 7 FIX rows, 7 numbered fixes, 5 missing ideas).\n4\tSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 175, 335, 336 and 404-414 with PyMuPDF (`staging/_tmp/f7a_text.txt`). I rendered PDF 175, 335, 336 and 404-411 and 414 to `staging/_renders/f7a_p<N>.png` and opened every page I quote from: 175 (3.4.2 Western and Russian after-effect studies), 335-336 (6.6.2 recording convention), 404 (five knowledge sources), 405 (superslow, active stretching, connective tissue, cyclical work), 406 (Fig 7.2 and \"optimal recovery intervals between sets\"), 407 (eccentrics and cheating warnings; no warning for forced reps), 408 (regimes, \"best known\", \"popularly used\", plateau \"commonly used\"), 409 (pyramids, interval training, 5RM minimum, 4-6RM, 10-20% strip), 410 (intensive clustering, segmental, hybrid), 411 (80³ over 3+5+2, contrast methods) and 414 (concluding remarks). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\n5\tScope: the 10 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch7a.txt`. I edited the 7 FIX entries plus the GO chart entry (missing ideas). No new entries, so the scope file is unchanged (10 lines). No renames. I did not edit `_book.md`, any 7b or 6d file, or the vault. Edits were made with an exact-match replacement script (`staging/_tmp/f7a_apply.py`; each replacement asserted to match once).\n6\t\n7\t| file | what changed |\n8\t|---|---|\n9\t| framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md | **Fix 1.** (1) \"Why `contested`\" now opens \"The rating applies only to the rest between near-maximal singles; the load-band definitions are not in dispute.\" (2) \"Both sentences describe\" is now \"As worded, both sentences describe\", followed by: PDF p. 406 calls the 1-5 minutes \"optimal recovery intervals between sets\", and the PDF p. 409 sentence opens the interval section that leads straight into cluster training (10-20 s within an extended set), \"so the two passages may describe different methods, sets of singles against clustered singles. The book does not say which, and does not reconcile them.\" (3) When NOT: \"the authors class eccentrics, forced reps and cheating as higher-risk methods\" is now \"the authors give injury warnings for eccentrics and cheating (PDF p. 407); forced repetitions sit in the same supramaximal group\" (PDF 407 image: warnings for eccentrics and cheating only). Beyond the review, for consistency: When NOT's \"two incompatible values\" is now \"two values that may describe different methods\". `contested` kept. |\n10\t| framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md | **Fix 2.** `confidence: untested` is now `supported`. \"Split routines and zone training suit athletes ...\" now starts \"Inference:\". |\n11\t| framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md | **Fix 3.** `confidence: untested` is now `supported`. When to apply: \"Inference: step sets can find a working load for a fixed repetition target.\" When NOT: \"Plateau sets are for novices and transition phases (PDF p. 408). Inference: not for a peaking block.\" The entry keeps the authors' \"Pyramiding is rarely used by serious athletes today\" caveat; `supported` rates the structures as established practice, and the caveat is the authors' own view on which structure serious athletes prefer. |\n12\t| framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md | **Fix 4.** \"Why `contested`\" now leads with the direct text: the same paragraph sets the minimum at \"one's 5RM\" (PDF p. 409) but prescribes extensive clustering \"with one's 4-6RM\" (a 6RM is lighter than a 5RM) and a stripping form that cuts the load \"by 10-20%\" after each set (PDF pp. 409-410), \"both of which take the load below that minimum\"; intensive clustering then gives \"75-90% of 1RM\" (PDF p. 410). The pyramid-based Inference is kept after it. When NOT now reads \"without choosing among its load statements (the 5RM minimum against the 4-6RM, the 10-20% strip and 75-90% of 1RM)\". Beyond the review, for consistency with fix 1: the \"second internal conflict\" paragraph now adds \"The two passages may describe different methods (sets of singles against clustered singles), and the book does not say which\". `contested` kept. |\n13\t| framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md | **Fix 5.** The \"Unclear as printed ... may be a set count\" note is replaced: \"By the 6.6.2 convention (PDF pp. 335-336 / printed pp. 324-325, checked against the page images, where \"60 x 10, 70 x 8, 80 x 6, 80 x 6\" is also written \"10(60)¹, 8(70)¹, 6(80)²\", so \"6(80)²\" means two sets of 6 at 80 kg) the superscript is the number of sets, so 80³ over 3+5+2 means three sets of the 10-repetition hybrid; the text's \"for a set of 3 repetitions\" conflicts with its own convention.\" When to apply now opens with the authors' use, \"When a circuit is to be condensed into one station (the authors' use, PDF p. 410)\", and the time/space clause follows as \"Inference: also when ...\". The Example now says \"written 80³ (three sets by the 6.6.2 convention)\". `untested` kept. |\n14\t| heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md | **Fix 6.** \"The random variant when a long block of one exercise has stalled\" is now \"The random variant from the start of a block, to limit early accommodation (the authors' stated purpose, PDF p. 410)\" (PDF 410 image: \"to minimise the possibility of early accommodation to a predictable regime\"). `untested` kept. |\n15\t| framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md | **Fix 7.** (1) \"Why `contested`\" now opens \"The rating applies to the heavy-light set.\" \"The heavy-light set and load release rest on ...\" is now \"The heavy-light set rests on exactly the effect these studies disagree about\", followed by \"Load release and pneumatics unload within a single repetition; the cited studies did not test that, so the conflict does not reach them directly.\" (2) The Russian findings on PDF 175 are quoted verbatim with their citation lists: \"Dynamic work with heavy weights (i.e. high tension with relatively few repetitions) also elicits a positive after-effect in the central nervous system (CNS), which produces a general toning influence on the motor apparatus and an improvement in speed and strength (Portnov, 1955; Ratov, 1957; Diachkov, 1961; Muravov & Tkachev, 1964; Letunov, 1965)\" and \"intense preliminary strength tension improves results in jumping exercises (Diachkov, 1958; 1961; Verkhoshansky, 1961; Tatian, 1964; Tkachev, 1967), in shotputting (Fritsch, 1961; Ivanova, 1964; Markov, 1966), and in rowing (Ermishkin & Vozniak, 1965; Chuprun, 1966)\". When NOT now reads \"Do not expect a reliable acute boost from the heavy-light set: the Western studies the book cites are split (two found a faster unloaded movement, others found none), and the Russian studies it cites found one (PDF p. 175).\" `contested` kept. |\n16\t| framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md | **Missing ideas 1-5** (GO entry). New paragraph \"Sources and scope of the catalogue\": the five sources (PDF 404), \"Only in recent years, has science begun to investigate seriously ...\" (PDF 404), and the concluding \"rapid reference for the major methods which are most frequently used in the training of high-level athletes\" plus the magazine-methods exclusion (PDF 414), with \"Inference: most of the 7.1 catalogue rests on practice in the strength sports, not on studies cited on these pages.\" New block \"Notes beside Fig 7.1\" (PDF 405) with four quoted bullets: superslow, active stretching, connective tissue first (Chapter 1 gives the basis and Chapter 6 the rule, both linked), low-intensity cyclical work. When NOT adds \"Do not read the catalogue as tested: the authors say science has only \"in recent years\" begun to examine Iron Game practice (PDF p. 404).\" Related adds five links (below). `untested` kept. |\n17\t\n18\t## Confidence rule, all 10 entries\n19\t\n20\tRule applied (coordinator decision): established, parameterised practice that the authors present with loads, reps, rest or other parameters is `supported`; anecdotes, novelties the authors discount, tip lists and orderings with no evidence are `untested`; `contested` stays where a same-question conflict is recorded. The book's own basis for the rule is the 7.1 concluding remark (PDF 414): the catalogue covers \"the major methods which are most frequently used in the training of high-level athletes\".\n21\t\n22\t| entry | before | after | reason |\n23\t|---|---|---|---|\n24\t| Figs 7.1 and 7.2 chart | untested | untested | A taxonomy. Its one empirical claim, the \"increasing importance of nervous system training\" arrow, has no evidence (PDF 405). |\n25\t| Maximal / supramaximal / circa-max / submax | contested | contested | Same-question conflict on rest between singles, now scoped to that rest figure only. |\n26\t| Three bodybuilding regimes | untested | **supported** | Fix 2. Sets, reps, load and rest given; \"one of the best known elementary bodybuilding methods\", add-ons \"popularly used\" (PDF 408). Same footing as Table 1.1. |\n27\t| Set structures | untested | **supported** | Fix 3. Parameterised; plateau \"commonly used\" (PDF 408), pyramid values \"derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9\" (PDF 409), stripping \"commonly used\" (PDF 408). |\n28\t| Interval training and cluster sets | contested | contested | Same-paragraph conflict on cluster load (fix 4). |\n29\t| Hybrid repetitions and sets | untested | untested | Repetition counts but no load or rest guidance; the examples \"may be devised by the inventive coach\" (PDF 411). The \"commonly used\" on PDF 411 refers to the Ch 8.4 list, not atomized here. |\n30\t| Progressive and random segmental | untested | untested | Twenty-Ones is \"A popular form\" with repetition counts (PDF 410), but no load or rest is given; the random variant is a named novelty (\"For want of a better term\"); Rigert is a single anecdote with no outcome. The rule does not clearly require a change. |\n31\t| Contrast methods | contested | contested | The heavy-light set is parameterised (20%, 10-20 s, 3-5 reps), but the recorded conflict on PDF 175 outranks that. |\n32\t| Miscellaneous bodybuilding sets | untested | untested | The authors call these \"of minimal significance\" for sport (PDF 412). |\n33\t| 27 sticking-point strategies | untested | untested | A tip list (\"Among some of the methods are the following\", PDF 413), no basis for most items. |\n34\t\n35\t## Missing ideas: placement\n36\t\n37\t| # | idea | placement |\n38\t|---|---|---|\n39\t| 1 | Superslow as a discrete quasi-isometric method (PDF 405) | Added to the chart entry (Fig 7.2 has a Quasi-isometrics box). No library entry, vault or staging, mentions \"superslow\" (grepped both). `framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology` defines quasi-isometric action but not the superslow method, so it is linked, not relied on. |\n40\t| 2 | Connective tissue first; low-intensity cyclical work in general preparation (PDF 405) | Half covered. Connective tissue: already held in `claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates` (Ch 1.6) and `principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue` (Ch 6.9.4); both are now linked from the chart entry, with the PDF 405 sentence quoted. Cyclical work: no entry covers it (grepped \"capillaris\", \"prolonged cyclical\"). I quoted it in the chart entry rather than writing a new entry: the source gives it in two sentences with no parameters, study, limit or example, so a standalone entry would need invented When NOT and Example sections. The coordinator can overrule. |\n41\t| 3 | Heavy full-range resistance as active stretching (PDF 405) | Added to the chart entry; linked `framework--stretching-techniques-taxonomy-static-vs-dynamic` and `heuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength`. |\n42\t| 4 | Concluding scope statement (PDF 414) | Added to the chart entry (\"Sources and scope\"), and used as the basis for the confidence rule above. |\n43\t| 5 | Chapter opening on knowledge sources (PDF 404) | Added to the chart entry (\"Sources and scope\"), plus a When NOT line. |\n44\t\n45\tNo new entry was written, so `_scope_supertraining_ch7a.txt` is unchanged.\n46\t\n47\t## Disagreements with the review\n48\t\n49\t1. **Fix 4: \"both fall below the stated minimum\" is narrowed.** The 4-6RM range is only partly below a 5RM (the 4RM and 5RM ends are at or above it). The entry says \"(a 6RM is lighter than a 5RM)\" and that the 4-6RM and the 10-20% strip \"take the load below that minimum\", which is what the text supports.\n50\t2. **Fix 7: the review's When NOT wording was not used literally.** \"the book reports Western studies that found none\" drops Murray (1959) and Van Huss et al (1962), which the same page (PDF 175) reports as a statistically significant increase. The entry now says the Western studies are split and the Russian studies found a boost. The review's \"Russian findings in favour\" sentence is applied with the full verbatim quotes and citation lists, as the task asked for exact wording.\n51\t3. **Fix 5: the convention is cited to PDF 335-336, not 336 alone.** The plain listing \"60 x 10, 70 x 8, 80 x 6, 80 x 6\" sits at the foot of PDF 335 (printed 324); the superscript form \"10(60)¹, 8(70)¹, 6(80)²\" heads PDF 336 (printed 325). Both images were opened. The review's reading (superscript = sets) holds.\n52\t4. **Beyond the review: two consistency edits.** The maximal entry's When NOT (\"two incompatible values\") and the interval entry's \"second internal conflict\" paragraph restated the rest-between-singles conflict unscoped; both now say the passages may describe different methods, to match fix 1.\n53\t\n54\tEvery other review claim held up on the page: no forced-reps injury warning on PDF 407; \"between sets\" on PDF 406; the 5RM, 4-6RM and 10-20% figures on PDF 409; the random variant's preventive purpose on PDF 410; \"time or space\" absent from PDF 410; 80³ over 3+5+2 on PDF 411; the Russian findings on PDF 175; \"best known\", \"popularly used\" and \"commonly used\" on PDF 408.\n55\t\n56\t## For the coordinator\n57\t\n58\t- Reciprocal links (outside 7a, not edited by me): `framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology`, `claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates`, `principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue`, `framework--stretching-techniques-taxonomy-static-vs-dynamic` and `heuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength` could link back to [[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]].\n59\t- Confidence tallies for 7a: before, `untested` 7 (chart, regimes, set structures, hybrid, segmental, miscellaneous, sticking points) and `contested` 3; after, `untested` 5, `supported` 2 (regimes, set structures), `contested` 3.\n60\t- No renames, so no 7b file links a changed slug.\n61\t\n62\t## Checks\n63\t\n64\tGrep proof, run from the book folder over the 10 scoped files (one or more lines per fixed file):\n65\t\n66\t```\n67\tframework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md:9:confidence: supported\n68\tframework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md:31:Inference: step sets can find\n69\tframework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md:33:Inference: not for a peaking block\n70\tframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:29:most frequently used in the training of high-level\n71\tframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:32:superslow method\n72\tframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:33:very effective means of active stretching\n73\tframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:34:Connective tissue first\n74\tframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:35:capillarisation in all the relevant\n75\tframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:41:Do not read the catalogue as tested\n76\tframework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md:36:The rating applies only to the rest between near-maximal singles\n77\tframework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md:36:optimal recovery intervals between sets\n78\tframework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md:42:injury warnings for eccentrics and cheating\n79\tframework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md:42:two values that may describe different methods\n80\tframework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md:9:confidence: supported\n81\tframework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md:37:Inference: split routines and zone training\n82\tframework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md:25:The rating applies to the heavy-light set\n83\tframework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md:25:Portnov, 1955; Ratov, 1957\n84\tframework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md:25:Ermishkin & Vozniak, 1965; Chuprun, 1966\n85\tframework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md:25:the conflict does not reach them directly\n86\tframework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md:29:the Western studies the book cites are split\n87\tframework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md:21:By the 6.6.2 convention\n88\tframework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md:21:three sets of the 10-repetition hybrid\n89\tframework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md:27:Inference: also when session time or space\n90\tframework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md:32:with one's 4-6RM\" (a 6RM is lighter\n91\tframework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md:34:may describe different methods (sets of singles\n92\tframework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md:42:the 10-20% strip and 75-90%\n93\theuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md:22:from the start of a block, to limit early accommodation\n94\t```\n95\t\n96\tOld phrases, count of scoped files still holding each:\n97\t\n98\t```\n99\tBoth sentences describe:        0\n100\tforced reps and cheating as higher-risk:        0\n101\ttwo incompatible values:        0\n102\tThe minimum load for cluster training is:        0\n103\tthe superscript may be a set count:        0\n104\tWhen session time or space is short:        0\n105\thas stalled:        1\n106\tThe heavy-light set and load release rest on:        0\n107\tfound none in several tasks:        0\n108\tbetween its two load statements:        0\n109\t```\n110\t\n111\tThe one \"has stalled\" hit is the sticking-point entry's own When to apply (\"When a lift has stalled for weeks ...\"), a GO entry and a different sentence; the segmental sentence is gone.\n112\t\n113\tMechanical greps over all 10 scoped files (run under `/bin/bash`):\n114\t\n115\t```\n116\t== em dashes not in section headers:\n117\t(end)\n118\t== em dashes in titles:\n119\t(end)\n120\t== review-history words:\n121\t(end)\n122\t== wrapped wikilinks:\n123\t(end)\n124\t== header count per file:\n125\t  10 6\n126\t== CR endings:\n127\t       0\n128\t== new link targets exist:\n129\tok framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology\n130\tok heuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength\n131\tok framework--stretching-techniques-taxonomy-static-vs-dynamic\n132\tok claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates\n133\tok principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue\n134\t```\n135\t\n136\tLint, run from the Flint root:\n137\t\n138\t```\n139\t$ ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky\n140\tsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 152, 153]\n141\tTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n142\t```\n143\t\n144\tNo 7a file appears in the lint output. The one issue is the known `_book.md` false positive (title and table dashes), outside scope.\n145\t-----\n146\t# Review: Supertraining Chapter 7 lead-in and 7.1 (10 entries), Opus independent reviewer\n147\t\n148\tDate: 2026-10-03. Scope: the 10 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch7a.txt`. Source: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 404-414 (printed 393-403), plus PDF 175 (printed 164), PDF 21-22 (printed 10-11), PDF 315 (printed 304) and PDF 335-336 (printed 324-325) for cross-references. PDF idx = printed + 11.\n149\t\n150\tHow checked: full text layer of PDF 404-414, 175, 21, 22, 315, 335-336. Page images opened for PDF 405, 406 (300 dpi, plus three full-resolution crops of Fig 7.2), 408, 409, 410, 411, 412, 413, 414 and 175 (renders in `staging/_renders/r7a_p*.png`). Every number quoted in the entries was matched against the page image as well as the text layer, because the text layer garbles some of them (\"3*6 sets\", \"1*3 repetitions\", \"I RM\", \"I Classical\" for \"A. Classical\"). All 13 \"Eric's baseline\" quotes were checked against `docs/method-baseline.md` lines 30, 41, 61, 76, 103, 104, 107, 109, 119, 142, 180, 182 and 186. Quotes, line numbers and provenance tags all match. Every `[[wikilink]]` in the 10 files resolves to a file in the vault or the staging library.\n151\t\n152\t## Confidence rule applied to all 10\n153\t\n154\tThe brief sets `supported` as the ceiling for training prescriptions and `untested` for anecdotes. The folder applies this unevenly. Table 1.1 (`framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal`, \"based largely on the practical experience of generations of bodybuilders and weightlifters\") and DeLorme/Oxford are `supported`. The Ch5 circuit-layout catalogue is `untested`. This review uses one rule:\n155\t\n156\t- **`supported`**: a prescription with parameters that the book presents as established practice in the strength sports, such as \"one of the best known\", \"commonly used\" or \"popularly used\". This matches the Table 1.1 entry.\n157\t- **`untested`**: a single anecdote; a bodybuilding novelty the authors themselves discount; a list of tips given without basis; or a taxonomy whose only empirical claim is an unevidenced ordering.\n158\t- **`contested`**: only where the entry shows a same-question conflict, and with the reason scoped to the claim actually in conflict.\n159\t\n160\t| Entry | Current | Deserves | Why |\n161\t|---|---|---|---|\n162\t| Figs 7.1 and 7.2 chart | untested | untested (keep) | A taxonomy. Its one empirical claim is the \"increasing importance of nervous system training\" arrow, which has no evidence (PDF 405). The deficit rule is rated `contested` in its own linked entry. |\n163\t| Maximal / supramaximal / circa-max / submax | contested | contested (keep, scope it) | The definitions themselves would be `supported`. Keeping `contested` follows the Ch1 lesson, provided the reason is scoped to the one rest figure (see FIX). |\n164\t| Three bodybuilding regimes | untested | **supported** | Parameterised prescriptions the book calls \"one of the best known elementary bodybuilding methods\", with add-ons \"popularly used\" (PDF 408). Same footing as Table 1.1. |\n165\t| Set structures | untested | **supported** | Parameterised prescriptions. Plateau is \"commonly used\" (PDF 408), pyramids carry %1RM values \"derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9\" (PDF 409), and DeLorme/APRE (rated `supported` and `contested` elsewhere) are named as pyramid forms. |\n166\t| Interval training and cluster sets | contested | contested (keep) | A real same-paragraph conflict on cluster load, and stronger than the entry shows (see FIX). |\n167\t| Hybrid repetitions and sets | untested | untested (keep) | The examples are the authors' own constructions (\"may be devised by the inventive coach\", PDF 411). No practice base or data is given beyond the clean-and-jerk. |\n168\t| Progressive and random segmental | untested | untested (keep) | One anecdote (Rigert) with no outcome reported. |\n169\t| Contrast methods | contested | contested (keep, scope it to heavy-light) | See FIX. |\n170\t| Miscellaneous bodybuilding sets | untested | untested (keep) | The authors frame these as novelties \"of minimal significance\" for sport (PDF 412). |\n171\t| 27 sticking-point strategies | untested | untested (keep) | A list of tips (\"Among some of the methods are the following\", PDF 413) with no basis given for most items. |\n172\t\n173\t## Voice\n174\t\n175\tNo first-person Siff passage and no third-person \"Verkhoshansky\" passage appears anywhere on PDF 404-414. The \"you\" in the sticking-point list (PDF 413-414) and in Peak Contraction Sets (PDF 413) is instructional second person, not authorial first person. All 10 entries therefore correctly write \"the authors\", as the brief requires when a passage cannot be assigned to one author. No entry presents Eric's or the writer's view as the authors'. The attribution problems listed below are unlabelled writer inferences.\n176\t\n177\t## Entry verdicts\n178\t\n179\t| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n180\t|---|---|---|---|\n181\t| framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md | **GO** | None found. | checked: Fig 7.1 boxes, caption and arrow direction against the PDF 405 image. Fig 7.2 at 300 dpi plus crops (PDF 406): all four column headers; 8 quality boxes, with connectors only from Explosive Strength (to Reactive), Flexibility (to the static/passive/active/ballistic/PNF box) and Hypertrophy (to Bodybuilding Methods), so \"five of eight have no drawn link\" is correct; Reactive connects to both Plyometrics and Contrast; Supramaximal runs straight to its variations box; Maximal, Circa-maximal and Submaximal have no connector; Quasi-isometrics and Endurance Methods have no variations; Oxford Method floats unlinked; Weightlifting, Isometrics, Pyramiding, Bodybuilding, Circuit and Multi-purpose each connect to their variation boxes; Stripping appears twice and APRE twice; every variation list matches. Quotes at PDF 404-405 / printed 393-394 and the concluding remark at PDF 414 / printed 403. Chapter 1 deficit quotes at PDF 21 / printed 10, including \"5RM to 8RM\". Baseline lines 76 and 103 quoted and tagged correctly. Rating `untested` is consistent with the rule above. |\n182\t| framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md | **FIX** | (1) The \"Why `contested`\" paragraph says \"Both sentences describe the rest between near-maximal singles in weightlifting training\". PDF 406 frames its 1-5 min as \"This 1RM set with optimal recovery intervals **between sets**\". The PDF 409 sentence (\"single repetitions of near-maximal weights with a few seconds of rest between repetitions\") opens the interval-training section and leads straight into Cluster Training (\"10-20 second interval between each repetition ... in an extended set\", PDF 409). So the two passages may describe different methods (sets of singles against clustered singles in one extended set). The conflict is real as worded, but it is not certain they answer the same question. (2) The entry carries `contested` because of one rest figure, but the entry is four categories of definitions, so a reader filtering by confidence would read the load bands as disputed. (3) \"When NOT to apply\" says \"the authors class eccentrics, forced reps and cheating as higher-risk methods (PDF p. 407)\". PDF 407 gives an injury warning for eccentrics (\"carries an increased risk of injury\") and for cheating (\"if injury is to be avoided\"), but none for forced repetitions. | (1) After the first sentence of \"Why `contested`\", add: \"PDF 406 calls the 1-5 minutes 'optimal recovery intervals between sets', and the PDF 409 sentence opens the interval section that leads straight into cluster training (10-20 s within an extended set), so the two passages may describe different methods, sets of singles against clustered singles. The book does not say which.\" Change \"Both sentences describe\" to \"As worded, both sentences describe\". (2) Start that paragraph with: \"The rating applies only to the rest between near-maximal singles; the load-band definitions are not in dispute.\" (3) Change to \"the authors give injury warnings for eccentrics and cheating (PDF p. 407); forced repetitions sit in the same supramaximal group\" or delete \"forced reps\". |\n183\t| framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md | **FIX** | (1) Confidence `untested` understates. These are parameterised prescriptions the book calls \"one of the best known elementary bodybuilding methods\" and \"popularly used\" (PDF 408 / printed 397), the same basis as the `supported` Table 1.1 entry. (2) \"When to apply\": \"Split routines and zone training suit athletes who lift on more days than a whole-body session allows.\" The authors say only that zone training \"must be spread over several sessions or days\" (PDF 409). The sentence sits after an \"Inference:\" sentence, so its label scope is unclear. Everything else checks: the table values (3-5 x 8-10, 2-5 min; 15-20 at 60-70%, 2-3 min; 5-8 at 80-90%, 2-5 min), the \"A.\" label (image), Table 1.1's 60-80 / 8-15 / 4-8 / 2-5 (PDF 22 / printed 11), the supersets, pre-exhaustion and split-week quotes, and baseline lines 61, 104 and 180. | (1) Set `confidence: supported`. (2) Prefix: \"Inference: split routines and zone training suit athletes who lift on more days than a whole-body session allows.\" |\n184\t| framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md | **FIX** | (1) Confidence `untested` understates, as above. Plateau is \"commonly used\" (PDF 408) and the pyramid values are \"derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9\" (PDF 409). (2) Unlabelled writer claims: \"Step sets to find a working load for a fixed repetition target\" (\"When to apply\"), and \"Plateau sets are for novices and transition phases, not for a peaking block\" (\"When NOT to apply\"). The authors give the plateau's use (PDF 408) but say nothing about peaking, and give no purpose for the step method. All numbers check against the PDF 408-409 images: 50x10/70x6 x3, 60-85 x3, the 5-10 kg strip, \"no more than 6 repetitions\", both pyramids, 2-6 min, 4 to 10, 2.5-5 kg, the \"rarely used by serious athletes\" quote and item 7 (PDF 413). | (1) Set `confidence: supported`. (2) Rewrite as \"Inference: step sets can find a working load for a fixed repetition target\" and \"Plateau sets are for novices and transition phases (PDF p. 408). Inference: not for a peaking block.\" |\n185\t| framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md | **FIX** | The contested reason is correct but incomplete, and it leans on the weaker evidence. The same paragraph that sets \"Characteristically, the minimum load used is one's 5RM\" (PDF 409 / printed 398, image) also prescribes extensive clustering \"with one's **4-6RM**\" (a 6RM is lighter than a 5RM) and a stripping variant where \"the load is decreased by 10-20%\" after each set (PDF 409-410). Both fall below the stated minimum in the text itself, without any %1RM conversion. The entry's 75-90% argument depends on reading the pyramid's \"6 x 85%\" as a 6RM, an inference (correctly labelled). All other values check against the PDF 409-410 images: 30-40% / 5-6 x 20-30 / 1-2 min; 50-60% / 3-6 sets to failure / 10-90 s; \">180 beats/min\"; circuits 1-3 min; \"75% of (220-Age)\". The Ch5 comparison (20-40 reps at ≤30% against 3-8 reps at >70%) checks at PDF 315 / printed 304. Baseline lines 119 and 182 are correct. | In \"Why `contested`\", lead with the direct text: \"The same paragraph sets the minimum at 'one's 5RM' (PDF p. 409) but prescribes extensive clustering 'with one's 4-6RM' and a stripping form that cuts the load 'by 10-20%' each set (PDF pp. 409-410), both below that minimum; intensive clustering then gives '75-90% of 1RM' (PDF p. 410).\" Keep the existing pyramid-based Inference after it as supporting reasoning. |\n186\t| framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md | **FIX** (minor) | (1) \"When to apply — When session time or space is short and several movements can be loaded by the same bar\". The authors say only \"Instead of doing three separate exercises at three different stations, one can do all of them in a single repetition or at a single station\" (PDF 410). Time and space are the writer's addition and are unlabelled. (2) The superscript is left \"Unclear as printed ... Inference: the superscript may be a set count\". The book settles this itself: the 6.6.2 convention writes 80 kg x 6 done twice as \"6(80)²\", with the superscript as the set count, and gives the hybrid example \"100 / 2+3+1\" (PDF 336 / printed 325). The PDF 411 image shows 80³ over 3+5+2, so the notation means 3 sets, and the book's \"for a set of 3 repetitions\" is a slip in its own wording. All other content checks against the PDF 410-411 images, including both example lists and the two guidelines. | (1) Prefix \"Inference:\" to the time/space clause, or replace it with \"When a circuit is to be condensed into one station (PDF p. 410).\" (2) Replace the unclear note with: \"By the 6.6.2 convention (PDF p. 336 / printed p. 325, where '6(80)²' means two sets of 6 at 80 kg) the superscript is the number of sets, so 80³ over 3+5+2 means three sets of the 10-repetition hybrid; the text's 'for a set of 3 repetitions' conflicts with its own convention.\" |\n187\t| heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md | **FIX** (minor) | \"When to apply\": \"The random variant when a long block of one exercise has stalled.\" The authors give the random variant a preventive purpose, \"to minimise the possibility of early accommodation to a predictable regime\" (PDF 410 / printed 399, image), not a remedy for a stall. The sentence sits outside the preceding \"Inference:\" sentence. All quotes check against the PDF 410 image, including \"2-4 segments\", \"3-7 repetitions\", Twenty-Ones, Rigert's three segments of 1-3 reps, and \"Frequently ... full clean pull\". | Replace with: \"The random variant from the start of a block, to limit early accommodation (the authors' stated purpose, PDF p. 410).\" If the stall use is wanted, write it as \"Inference: ...\". |\n188\t| framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md | **FIX** | (1) Scope. The entry says \"The heavy-light set and load release rest on exactly the effect those studies dispute.\" The 3.4.2 Western studies test \"the speed of an unloaded movement ... directly after the same movement was executed with a weight\" (PDF 175 / printed 164, image). That is a preceding loaded effort followed by a lighter one, which is the heavy-light set. Load release and pneumatics drop the load inside one repetition. The authors give a different mechanism for them (\"the contrast in stimulus ... produces a rapid acceleration\", PDF 411), and the cited studies did not test it. (2) The evidence is reported one-sided. The same page, PDF 175, reports the Russian side: \"Dynamic work with heavy weights (i.e. high tension with relatively few repetitions) also elicits a positive after-effect in the central nervous system ... and an improvement in speed and strength\" (Portnov 1955 and others), and \"intense preliminary strength tension improves results in jumping exercises ... in shotputting ... and in rowing\". \"When NOT to apply\" says only \"the book's own cited Western studies found none in several tasks\". That is the one-sided reading the Ch1 lesson forbids. All method definitions and the 20% / 10-20 s / 3-5 rep figures check against the PDF 411 image, and the Ch5 shock-method link (contrast methods listed, \"unsuitable for novices\") checks. | (1) In \"Why `contested`\", change \"The heavy-light set and load release rest on\" to \"The heavy-light set rests on\". Add: \"Load release and pneumatics unload within a single repetition; the cited studies did not test that, so the conflict does not reach them directly.\" (2) Add after the Western list: \"The same page reports Russian findings in favour: heavy dynamic work 'elicits a positive after-effect ... and an improvement in speed and strength', and intense preliminary strength tension improves jumping, shot-put and rowing results (PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164).\" In \"When NOT to apply\", change the first sentence to: \"Do not expect a reliable acute boost: the book reports Western studies that found none and Russian studies that found one (PDF p. 175).\" |\n189\t| framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations.md | **GO** | None found. | checked: the \"Miscellaneous Methods\" heading heads PDF 412 (image), followed by the frame quote (\"of minimal significance\", \"shock the body ... out of any prevailing state of habituation or stagnation\"); every method definition on PDF 412-413 (images), including \"30-25-20-15-10\", \"10-12 sets\", \"4-5 sets\", \"10RM, with 10 second rest intervals\" and the functional-priority order; Continuous Tension Sets at PDF 410 (image); the PHA quotes and \"Setchinov\" at PDF 412; the 5.6 Setchinov entry does use the effect for restoration between stations; baseline lines 41 and 109 quoted and tagged correctly. Every Inference is labelled. |\n190\t| heuristic--overcoming-sticking-points-repertoire-of-27-strategies.md | **GO** | None found. | checked: PDF 413-414 images. Items 1-18 run on PDF 413 into 414, then 20-28. There is no item 19, so the list holds 27. The entry's six groups contain every one of the 27 numbers exactly once (1,2,3,7 / 4,6,8 / 5,13 / 9,11,20,21,26,27,28 / 10,15,16,17,18,25 / 12,14,22,23,24). The entry paraphrases with short quotes and does not reprint the list. Checked \"80 - 92.5 - 97.5 - 102.5kg\", \"60 - 80 - 100kg\", \"0.5 - 1.25kg\", the item 12 competition caveat, the item 13 rationale, and the opening quote including the sic \"one of single most\". Baseline lines 30, 61 and 142 quoted and tagged correctly. The Ch5 overtraining caution is cited to its own entry and labelled as not from this list. |\n191\t\n192\tCounts: **GO 3, FIX 7, NO-GO 0.**\n193\t\n194\t## Duplicates (check 5)\n195\t\n196\tNone. Grepped the vault library and the staging library for: cluster, heavy-light, rest-pause, pyramid, twenty-one / segmental, hybrid, sticking point, peripheral heart / PHA, drop set / stripping, superset, pre-exhaust, superslow. The relevant hits cover different angles and are already linked from these entries:\n197\t\n198\t- circuit layouts (superset / stripping circuits)\n199\t- parachute contrast release\n200\t- APRE and DeLorme pyramids\n201\t- Setchinov station sequencing\n202\t- Zatsiorsky's joint-angle \"sticking point\"\n203\t- the Ch3 interval-style energy cost\n204\t\n205\tThe existing CAT entry already covers Compensatory Deceleration and Compensatory Action Training from PDF 411, so leaving CAT out of this batch is correct.\n206\t\n207\t## Missing (check 6)\n208\t\n209\t1. **Quasi-isometrics as a discrete \"superslow\" method** (PDF 405 / printed 394). Quasi-isometrics is unavoidable with near-maximal loads, but as a chosen method it means very slow repetitions with moderate or light loads, \"popularised in bodybuilding circles as the superslow method\". No library entry mentions superslow. It could be a short addition to the Fig 7.1/7.2 entry, which already lists Quasi-isometrics as a box.\n210\t2. **Connective tissue first, plus low-intensity cyclical work in general preparation** (PDF 405 / printed 394). \"Great emphasis should be placed on increasing the mass and strength of the connective tissues ... particularly during the early stages of training and transition phases\", and low-intensity prolonged cyclical activity is \"an essential part of the general preparatory phase of all training\", also useful for restoration. This is a programming rule, not a method, and no entry in this batch captures it. Check `principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue` for overlap before writing a new entry.\n211\t3. **Heavy full-range resistance as active stretching** (PDF 405). \"Several of the other training methods with heavy resistance used over a full range of movement constitute very effective means of active stretching.\" This could be one line in the chart entry or a link from a flexibility entry.\n212\t4. **Concluding scope statement** (PDF 414 / printed 403). The summary covers \"the major methods which are most frequently used in the training of high-level athletes\", and leaves out magazine methods that \"differ only in name and a few minor details\". This is useful context for the confidence of the whole 7.1 catalogue: the authors present it as frequently used practice. It could go in `_book.md` or the chart entry.\n213\t5. **Chapter opening on knowledge sources** (PDF 404 / printed 393). It lists five sources (weightlifting and powerlifting, bodybuilding, supplementary resistance training, physical therapy, scientific research) and says science only recently began testing Iron Game practice. This explains why most of 7.1 rests on practice, not studies. It is low priority, and one sentence in the chart entry would cover it.\n214\t\n215\t## Overall verdict\n216\t\n217\t**GO AFTER FIXES.** Required fixes:\n218\t\n219\t1. **Maximal methods:** scope the `contested` reason to the single rest figure, add the \"between sets\" against cluster-context caveat, and drop or correct \"forced reps\" as a higher-risk method with a stated warning.\n220\t2. **Three regimes:** set `confidence: supported`, and label the split-routine/zone sentence as Inference.\n221\t3. **Set structures:** set `confidence: supported`, and label the step-method purpose and \"not for a peaking block\" as Inference.\n222\t4. **Interval and cluster:** add the direct same-paragraph contradictions (4-6RM; the 10-20% strip) to the `contested` reason.\n223\t5. **Hybrid:** label the time/space clause, and resolve the superscript with the PDF 336 / printed 325 convention (it is a set count).\n224\t6. **Segmental:** restate the random variant's use as preventive (PDF 410), or label the stall use as Inference.\n225\t7. **Contrast:** scope `contested` to the heavy-light set (not load release or pneumatics), and report the Russian positive findings from the same PDF 175 page in both \"Why `contested`\" and \"When NOT to apply\".\n226\t\n227\tFixes 2 and 3 apply the confidence rule stated above. If Eric prefers to keep catalogue entries at `untested`, apply that consistently to the `supported` Table 1.1 entry as well, rather than leaving the folder split.\n228\t"}]},"uuid":"ce3367d3-6c7b-4a52-ae04-479fde234279","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:28:50.006Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tool-results/bn9boah4j.txt","content":"# Fixes applied: Supertraining Chapter 7 lead-in and 7.1 (Ch7a), 10 entries\n\nFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch7a_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES: 7 FIX rows, 7 numbered fixes, 5 missing ideas).\nSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 175, 335, 336 and 404-414 with PyMuPDF (`staging/_tmp/f7a_text.txt`). I rendered PDF 175, 335, 336 and 404-411 and 414 to `staging/_renders/f7a_p<N>.png` and opened every page I quote from: 175 (3.4.2 Western and Russian after-effect studies), 335-336 (6.6.2 recording convention), 404 (five knowledge sources), 405 (superslow, active stretching, connective tissue, cyclical work), 406 (Fig 7.2 and \"optimal recovery intervals between sets\"), 407 (eccentrics and cheating warnings; no warning for forced reps), 408 (regimes, \"best known\", \"popularly used\", plateau \"commonly used\"), 409 (pyramids, interval training, 5RM minimum, 4-6RM, 10-20% strip), 410 (intensive clustering, segmental, hybrid), 411 (80³ over 3+5+2, contrast methods) and 414 (concluding remarks). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\nScope: the 10 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch7a.txt`. I edited the 7 FIX entries plus the GO chart entry (missing ideas). No new entries, so the scope file is unchanged (10 lines). No renames. I did not edit `_book.md`, any 7b or 6d file, or the vault. Edits were made with an exact-match replacement script (`staging/_tmp/f7a_apply.py`; each replacement asserted to match once).\n\n| file | what changed |\n|---|---|\n| framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md | **Fix 1.** (1) \"Why `contested`\" now opens \"The rating applies only to the rest between near-maximal singles; the load-band definitions are not in dispute.\" (2) \"Both sentences describe\" is now \"As worded, both sentences describe\", followed by: PDF p. 406 calls the 1-5 minutes \"optimal recovery intervals between sets\", and the PDF p. 409 sentence opens the interval section that leads straight into cluster training (10-20 s within an extended set), \"so the two passages may describe different methods, sets of singles against clustered singles. The book does not say which, and does not reconcile them.\" (3) When NOT: \"the authors class eccentrics, forced reps and cheating as higher-risk methods\" is now \"the authors give injury warnings for eccentrics and cheating (PDF p. 407); forced repetitions sit in the same supramaximal group\" (PDF 407 image: warnings for eccentrics and cheating only). Beyond the review, for consistency: When NOT's \"two incompatible values\" is now \"two values that may describe different methods\". `contested` kept. |\n| framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md | **Fix 2.** `confidence: untested` is now `supported`. \"Split routines and zone training suit athletes ...\" now starts \"Inference:\". |\n| framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md | **Fix 3.** `confidence: untested` is now `supported`. When to apply: \"Inference: step sets can find a working load for a fixed repetition target.\" When NOT: \"Plateau sets are for novices and transition phases (PDF p. 408). Inference: not for a peaking block.\" The entry keeps the authors' \"Pyramiding is rarely used by serious athletes today\" caveat; `supported` rates the structures as established practice, and the caveat is the authors' own view on which structure serious athletes prefer. |\n| framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md | **Fix 4.** \"Why `contested`\" now leads with the direct text: the same paragraph sets the minimum at \"one's 5RM\" (PDF p. 409) but prescribes extensive clustering \"with one's 4-6RM\" (a 6RM is lighter than a 5RM) and a stripping form that cuts the load \"by 10-20%\" after each set (PDF pp. 409-410), \"both of which take the load below that minimum\"; intensive clustering then gives \"75-90% of 1RM\" (PDF p. 410). The pyramid-based Inference is kept after it. When NOT now reads \"without choosing among its load statements (the 5RM minimum against the 4-6RM, the 10-20% strip and 75-90% of 1RM)\". Beyond the review, for consistency with fix 1: the \"second internal conflict\" paragraph now adds \"The two passages may describe different methods (sets of singles against clustered singles), and the book does not say which\". `contested` kept. |\n| framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md | **Fix 5.** The \"Unclear as printed ... may be a set count\" note is replaced: \"By the 6.6.2 convention (PDF pp. 335-336 / printed pp. 324-325, checked against the page images, where \"60 x 10, 70 x 8, 80 x 6, 80 x 6\" is also written \"10(60)¹, 8(70)¹, 6(80)²\", so \"6(80)²\" means two sets of 6 at 80 kg) the superscript is the number of sets, so 80³ over 3+5+2 means three sets of the 10-repetition hybrid; the text's \"for a set of 3 repetitions\" conflicts with its own convention.\" When to apply now opens with the authors' use, \"When a circuit is to be condensed into one station (the authors' use, PDF p. 410)\", and the time/space clause follows as \"Inference: also when ...\". The Example now says \"written 80³ (three sets by the 6.6.2 convention)\". `untested` kept. |\n| heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md | **Fix 6.** \"The random variant when a long block of one exercise has stalled\" is now \"The random variant from the start of a block, to limit early accommodation (the authors' stated purpose, PDF p. 410)\" (PDF 410 image: \"to minimise the possibility of early accommodation to a predictable regime\"). `untested` kept. |\n| framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md | **Fix 7.** (1) \"Why `contested`\" now opens \"The rating applies to the heavy-light set.\" \"The heavy-light set and load release rest on ...\" is now \"The heavy-light set rests on exactly the effect these studies disagree about\", followed by \"Load release and pneumatics unload within a single repetition; the cited studies did not test that, so the conflict does not reach them directly.\" (2) The Russian findings on PDF 175 are quoted verbatim with their citation lists: \"Dynamic work with heavy weights (i.e. high tension with relatively few repetitions) also elicits a positive after-effect in the central nervous system (CNS), which produces a general toning influence on the motor apparatus and an improvement in speed and strength (Portnov, 1955; Ratov, 1957; Diachkov, 1961; Muravov & Tkachev, 1964; Letunov, 1965)\" and \"intense preliminary strength tension improves results in jumping exercises (Diachkov, 1958; 1961; Verkhoshansky, 1961; Tatian, 1964; Tkachev, 1967), in shotputting (Fritsch, 1961; Ivanova, 1964; Markov, 1966), and in rowing (Ermishkin & Vozniak, 1965; Chuprun, 1966)\". When NOT now reads \"Do not expect a reliable acute boost from the heavy-light set: the Western studies the book cites are split (two found a faster unloaded movement, others found none), and the Russian studies it cites found one (PDF p. 175).\" `contested` kept. |\n| framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md | **Missing ideas 1-5** (GO entry). New paragraph \"Sources and scope of the catalogue\": the five sources (PDF 404), \"Only in recent years, has science begun to investigate seriously ...\" (PDF 404), and the concluding \"rapid reference for the major methods which are most frequently used in the training of high-level athletes\" plus the magazine-methods exclusion (PDF 414), with \"Inference: most of the 7.1 catalogue rests on practice in the strength sports, not on studies cited on these pages.\" New block \"Notes beside Fig 7.1\" (PDF 405) with four quoted bullets: superslow, active stretching, connective tissue first (Chapter 1 gives the basis and Chapter 6 the rule, both linked), low-intensity cyclical work. When NOT adds \"Do not read the catalogue as tested: the authors say science has only \"in recent years\" begun to examine Iron Game practice (PDF p. 404).\" Related adds five links (below). `untested` kept. |\n\n## Confidence rule, all 10 entries\n\nRule applied (coordinator decision): established, parameterised practice that the authors present with loads, reps, rest or other parameters is `supported`; anecdotes, novelties the authors discount, tip lists and orderings with no evidence are `untested`; `contested` stays where a same-question conflict is recorded. The book's own basis for the rule is the 7.1 concluding remark (PDF 414): the catalogue covers \"the major methods which are most frequently used in the training of high-level athletes\".\n\n| entry | before | after | reason |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Figs 7.1 and 7.2 chart | untested | untested | A taxonomy. Its one empirical claim, the \"increasing importance of nervous system training\" arrow, has no evidence (PDF 405). |\n| Maximal / supramaximal / circa-max / submax | contested | contested | Same-question conflict on rest between singles, now scoped to that rest figure only. |\n| Three bodybuilding regimes | untested | **supported** | Fix 2. Sets, reps, load and rest given; \"one of the best known elementary bodybuilding methods\", add-ons \"popularly used\" (PDF 408). Same footing as Table 1.1. |\n| Set structures | untested | **supported** | Fix 3. Parameterised; plateau \"commonly used\" (PDF 408), pyramid values \"derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9\" (PDF 409), stripping \"commonly used\" (PDF 408). |\n| Interval training and cluster sets | contested | contested | Same-paragraph conflict on cluster load (fix 4). |\n| Hybrid repetitions and sets | untested | untested | Repetition counts but no load or rest guidance; the examples \"may be devised by the inventive coach\" (PDF 411). The \"commonly used\" on PDF 411 refers to the Ch 8.4 list, not atomized here. |\n| Progressive and random segmental | untested | untested | Twenty-Ones is \"A popular form\" with repetition counts (PDF 410), but no load or rest is given; the random variant is a named novelty (\"For want of a better term\"); Rigert is a single anecdote with no outcome. The rule does not clearly require a change. |\n| Contrast methods | contested | contested | The heavy-light set is parameterised (20%, 10-20 s, 3-5 reps), but the recorded conflict on PDF 175 outranks that. |\n| Miscellaneous bodybuilding sets | untested | untested | The authors call these \"of minimal significance\" for sport (PDF 412). |\n| 27 sticking-point strategies | untested | untested | A tip list (\"Among some of the methods are the following\", PDF 413), no basis for most items. |\n\n## Missing ideas: placement\n\n| # | idea | placement |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Superslow as a discrete quasi-isometric method (PDF 405) | Added to the chart entry (Fig 7.2 has a Quasi-isometrics box). No library entry, vault or staging, mentions \"superslow\" (grepped both). `framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology` defines quasi-isometric action but not the superslow method, so it is linked, not relied on. |\n| 2 | Connective tissue first; low-intensity cyclical work in general preparation (PDF 405) | Half covered. Connective tissue: already held in `claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates` (Ch 1.6) and `principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue` (Ch 6.9.4); both are now linked from the chart entry, with the PDF 405 sentence quoted. Cyclical work: no entry covers it (grepped \"capillaris\", \"prolonged cyclical\"). I quoted it in the chart entry rather than writing a new entry: the source gives it in two sentences with no parameters, study, limit or example, so a standalone entry would need invented When NOT and Example sections. The coordinator can overrule. |\n| 3 | Heavy full-range resistance as active stretching (PDF 405) | Added to the chart entry; linked `framework--stretching-techniques-taxonomy-static-vs-dynamic` and `heuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength`. |\n| 4 | Concluding scope statement (PDF 414) | Added to the chart entry (\"Sources and scope\"), and used as the basis for the confidence rule above. |\n| 5 | Chapter opening on knowledge sources (PDF 404) | Added to the chart entry (\"Sources and scope\"), plus a When NOT line. |\n\nNo new entry was written, so `_scope_supertraining_ch7a.txt` is unchanged.\n\n## Disagreements with the review\n\n1. **Fix 4: \"both fall below the stated minimum\" is narrowed.** The 4-6RM range is only partly below a 5RM (the 4RM and 5RM ends are at or above it). The entry says \"(a 6RM is lighter than a 5RM)\" and that the 4-6RM and the 10-20% strip \"take the load below that minimum\", which is what the text supports.\n2. **Fix 7: the review's When NOT wording was not used literally.** \"the book reports Western studies that found none\" drops Murray (1959) and Van Huss et al (1962), which the same page (PDF 175) reports as a statistically significant increase. The entry now says the Western studies are split and the Russian studies found a boost. The review's \"Russian findings in favour\" sentence is applied with the full verbatim quotes and citation lists, as the task asked for exact wording.\n3. **Fix 5: the convention is cited to PDF 335-336, not 336 alone.** The plain listing \"60 x 10, 70 x 8, 80 x 6, 80 x 6\" sits at the foot of PDF 335 (printed 324); the superscript form \"10(60)¹, 8(70)¹, 6(80)²\" heads PDF 336 (printed 325). Both images were opened. The review's reading (superscript = sets) holds.\n4. **Beyond the review: two consistency edits.** The maximal entry's When NOT (\"two incompatible values\") and the interval entry's \"second internal conflict\" paragraph restated the rest-between-singles conflict unscoped; both now say the passages may describe different methods, to match fix 1.\n\nEvery other review claim held up on the page: no forced-reps injury warning on PDF 407; \"between sets\" on PDF 406; the 5RM, 4-6RM and 10-20% figures on PDF 409; the random variant's preventive purpose on PDF 410; \"time or space\" absent from PDF 410; 80³ over 3+5+2 on PDF 411; the Russian findings on PDF 175; \"best known\", \"popularly used\" and \"commonly used\" on PDF 408.\n\n## For the coordinator\n\n- Reciprocal links (outside 7a, not edited by me): `framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology`, `claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates`, `principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue`, `framework--stretching-techniques-taxonomy-static-vs-dynamic` and `heuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength` could link back to [[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]].\n- Confidence tallies for 7a: before, `untested` 7 (chart, regimes, set structures, hybrid, segmental, miscellaneous, sticking points) and `contested` 3; after, `untested` 5, `supported` 2 (regimes, set structures), `contested` 3.\n- No renames, so no 7b file links a changed slug.\n\n## Checks\n\nGrep proof, run from the book folder over the 10 scoped files (one or more lines per fixed file):\n\n```\nframework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md:9:confidence: supported\nframework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md:31:Inference: step sets can find\nframework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md:33:Inference: not for a peaking block\nframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:29:most frequently used in the training of high-level\nframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:32:superslow method\nframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:33:very effective means of active stretching\nframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:34:Connective tissue first\nframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:35:capillarisation in all the relevant\nframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:41:Do not read the catalogue as tested\nframework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md:36:The rating applies only to the rest between near-maximal singles\nframework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md:36:optimal recovery intervals between sets\nframework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md:42:injury warnings for eccentrics and cheating\nframework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md:42:two values that may describe different methods\nframework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md:9:confidence: supported\nframework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md:37:Inference: split routines and zone training\nframework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md:25:The rating applies to the heavy-light set\nframework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md:25:Portnov, 1955; Ratov, 1957\nframework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md:25:Ermishkin & Vozniak, 1965; Chuprun, 1966\nframework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md:25:the conflict does not reach them directly\nframework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md:29:the Western studies the book cites are split\nframework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md:21:By the 6.6.2 convention\nframework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md:21:three sets of the 10-repetition hybrid\nframework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md:27:Inference: also when session time or space\nframework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md:32:with one's 4-6RM\" (a 6RM is lighter\nframework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md:34:may describe different methods (sets of singles\nframework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md:42:the 10-20% strip and 75-90%\nheuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md:22:from the start of a block, to limit early accommodation\n```\n\nOld phrases, count of scoped files still holding each:\n\n```\nBoth sentences describe:        0\nforced reps and cheating as higher-risk:        0\ntwo incompatible values:        0\nThe minimum load for cluster training is:        0\nthe superscript may be a set count:        0\nWhen session time or space is short:        0\nhas stalled:        1\nThe heavy-light set and load release rest on:        0\nfound none in several tasks:        0\nbetween its two load statements:        0\n```\n\nThe one \"has stalled\" hit is the sticking-point entry's own When to apply (\"When a lift has stalled for weeks ...\"), a GO entry and a different sentence; the segmental sentence is gone.\n\nMechanical greps over all 10 scoped files (run under `/bin/bash`):\n\n```\n== em dashes not in section headers:\n(end)\n== em dashes in titles:\n(end)\n== review-history words:\n(end)\n== wrapped wikilinks:\n(end)\n== header count per file:\n  10 6\n== CR endings:\n       0\n== new link targets exist:\nok framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology\nok heuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength\nok framework--stretching-techniques-taxonomy-static-vs-dynamic\nok claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates\nok principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue\n```\n\nLint, run from the Flint root:\n\n```\n$ ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 152, 153]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n```\n\nNo 7a file appears in the lint output. The one issue is the known `_book.md` false positive (title and table dashes), outside scope.\n-----\n# Review: Supertraining Chapter 7 lead-in and 7.1 (10 entries), Opus independent reviewer\n\nDate: 2026-10-03. Scope: the 10 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch7a.txt`. Source: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 404-414 (printed 393-403), plus PDF 175 (printed 164), PDF 21-22 (printed 10-11), PDF 315 (printed 304) and PDF 335-336 (printed 324-325) for cross-references. PDF idx = printed + 11.\n\nHow checked: full text layer of PDF 404-414, 175, 21, 22, 315, 335-336. Page images opened for PDF 405, 406 (300 dpi, plus three full-resolution crops of Fig 7.2), 408, 409, 410, 411, 412, 413, 414 and 175 (renders in `staging/_renders/r7a_p*.png`). Every number quoted in the entries was matched against the page image as well as the text layer, because the text layer garbles some of them (\"3*6 sets\", \"1*3 repetitions\", \"I RM\", \"I Classical\" for \"A. Classical\"). All 13 \"Eric's baseline\" quotes were checked against `docs/method-baseline.md` lines 30, 41, 61, 76, 103, 104, 107, 109, 119, 142, 180, 182 and 186. Quotes, line numbers and provenance tags all match. Every `[[wikilink]]` in the 10 files resolves to a file in the vault or the staging library.\n\n## Confidence rule applied to all 10\n\nThe brief sets `supported` as the ceiling for training prescriptions and `untested` for anecdotes. The folder applies this unevenly. Table 1.1 (`framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal`, \"based largely on the practical experience of generations of bodybuilders and weightlifters\") and DeLorme/Oxford are `supported`. The Ch5 circuit-layout catalogue is `untested`. This review uses one rule:\n\n- **`supported`**: a prescription with parameters that the book presents as established practice in the strength sports, such as \"one of the best known\", \"commonly used\" or \"popularly used\". This matches the Table 1.1 entry.\n- **`untested`**: a single anecdote; a bodybuilding novelty the authors themselves discount; a list of tips given without basis; or a taxonomy whose only empirical claim is an unevidenced ordering.\n- **`contested`**: only where the entry shows a same-question conflict, and with the reason scoped to the claim actually in conflict.\n\n| Entry | Current | Deserves | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Figs 7.1 and 7.2 chart | untested | untested (keep) | A taxonomy. Its one empirical claim is the \"increasing importance of nervous system training\" arrow, which has no evidence (PDF 405). The deficit rule is rated `contested` in its own linked entry. |\n| Maximal / supramaximal / circa-max / submax | contested | contested (keep, scope it) | The definitions themselves would be `supported`. Keeping `contested` follows the Ch1 lesson, provided the reason is scoped to the one rest figure (see FIX). |\n| Three bodybuilding regimes | untested | **supported** | Parameterised prescriptions the book calls \"one of the best known elementary bodybuilding methods\", with add-ons \"popularly used\" (PDF 408). Same footing as Table 1.1. |\n| Set structures | untested | **supported** | Parameterised prescriptions. Plateau is \"commonly used\" (PDF 408), pyramids carry %1RM values \"derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9\" (PDF 409), and DeLorme/APRE (rated `supported` and `contested` elsewhere) are named as pyramid forms. |\n| Interval training and cluster sets | contested | contested (keep) | A real same-paragraph conflict on cluster load, and stronger than the entry shows (see FIX). |\n| Hybrid repetitions and sets | untested | untested (keep) | The examples are the authors' own constructions (\"may be devised by the inventive coach\", PDF 411). No practice base or data is given beyond the clean-and-jerk. |\n| Progressive and random segmental | untested | untested (keep) | One anecdote (Rigert) with no outcome reported. |\n| Contrast methods | contested | contested (keep, scope it to heavy-light) | See FIX. |\n| Miscellaneous bodybuilding sets | untested | untested (keep) | The authors frame these as novelties \"of minimal significance\" for sport (PDF 412). |\n| 27 sticking-point strategies | untested | untested (keep) | A list of tips (\"Among some of the methods are the following\", PDF 413) with no basis given for most items. |\n\n## Voice\n\nNo first-person Siff passage and no third-person \"Verkhoshansky\" passage appears anywhere on PDF 404-414. The \"you\" in the sticking-point list (PDF 413-414) and in Peak Contraction Sets (PDF 413) is instructional second person, not authorial first person. All 10 entries therefore correctly write \"the authors\", as the brief requires when a passage cannot be assigned to one author. No entry presents Eric's or the writer's view as the authors'. The attribution problems listed below are unlabelled writer inferences.\n\n## Entry verdicts\n\n| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md | **GO** | None found. | checked: Fig 7.1 boxes, caption and arrow direction against the PDF 405 image. Fig 7.2 at 300 dpi plus crops (PDF 406): all four column headers; 8 quality boxes, with connectors only from Explosive Strength (to Reactive), Flexibility (to the static/passive/active/ballistic/PNF box) and Hypertrophy (to Bodybuilding Methods), so \"five of eight have no drawn link\" is correct; Reactive connects to both Plyometrics and Contrast; Supramaximal runs straight to its variations box; Maximal, Circa-maximal and Submaximal have no connector; Quasi-isometrics and Endurance Methods have no variations; Oxford Method floats unlinked; Weightlifting, Isometrics, Pyramiding, Bodybuilding, Circuit and Multi-purpose each connect to their variation boxes; Stripping appears twice and APRE twice; every variation list matches. Quotes at PDF 404-405 / printed 393-394 and the concluding remark at PDF 414 / printed 403. Chapter 1 deficit quotes at PDF 21 / printed 10, including \"5RM to 8RM\". Baseline lines 76 and 103 quoted and tagged correctly. Rating `untested` is consistent with the rule above. |\n| framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md | **FIX** | (1) The \"Why `contested`\" paragraph says \"Both sentences describe the rest between near-maximal singles in weightlifting training\". PDF 406 frames its 1-5 min as \"This 1RM set with optimal recovery intervals **between sets**\". The PDF 409 sentence (\"single repetitions of near-maximal weights with a few seconds of rest between repetitions\") opens the interval-training section and leads straight into Cluster Training (\"10-20 second interval between each repetition ... in an extended set\", PDF 409). So the two passages may describe different methods (sets of singles against clustered singles in one extended set). The conflict is real as worded, but it is not certain they answer the same question. (2) The entry carries `contested` because of one rest figure, but the entry is four categories of definitions, so a reader filtering by confidence would read the load bands as disputed. (3) \"When NOT to apply\" says \"the authors class eccentrics, forced reps and cheating as higher-risk methods (PDF p. 407)\". PDF 407 gives an injury warning for eccentrics (\"carries an increased risk of injury\") and for cheating (\"if injury is to be avoided\"), but none for forced repetitions. | (1) After the first sentence of \"Why `contested`\", add: \"PDF 406 calls the 1-5 minutes 'optimal recovery intervals between sets', and the PDF 409 sentence opens the interval section that leads straight into cluster training (10-20 s within an extended set), so the two passages may describe different methods, sets of singles against clustered singles. The book does not say which.\" Change \"Both sentences describe\" to \"As worded, both sentences describe\". (2) Start that paragraph with: \"The rating applies only to the rest between near-maximal singles; the load-band definitions are not in dispute.\" (3) Change to \"the authors give injury warnings for eccentrics and cheating (PDF p. 407); forced repetitions sit in the same supramaximal group\" or delete \"forced reps\". |\n| framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md | **FIX** | (1) Confidence `untested` understates. These are parameterised prescriptions the book calls \"one of the best known elementary bodybuilding methods\" and \"popularly used\" (PDF 408 / printed 397), the same basis as the `supported` Table 1.1 entry. (2) \"When to apply\": \"Split routines and zone training suit athletes who lift on more days than a whole-body session allows.\" The authors say only that zone training \"must be spread over several sessions or days\" (PDF 409). The sentence sits after an \"Inference:\" sentence, so its label scope is unclear. Everything else checks: the table values (3-5 x 8-10, 2-5 min; 15-20 at 60-70%, 2-3 min; 5-8 at 80-90%, 2-5 min), the \"A.\" label (image), Table 1.1's 60-80 / 8-15 / 4-8 / 2-5 (PDF 22 / printed 11), the supersets, pre-exhaustion and split-week quotes, and baseline lines 61, 104 and 180. | (1) Set `confidence: supported`. (2) Prefix: \"Inference: split routines and zone training suit athletes who lift on more days than a whole-body session allows.\" |\n| framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md | **FIX** | (1) Confidence `untested` understates, as above. Plateau is \"commonly used\" (PDF 408) and the pyramid values are \"derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9\" (PDF 409). (2) Unlabelled writer claims: \"Step sets to find a working load for a fixed repetition target\" (\"When to apply\"), and \"Plateau sets are for novices and transition phases, not for a peaking block\" (\"When NOT to apply\"). The authors give the plateau's use (PDF 408) but say nothing about peaking, and give no purpose for the step method. All numbers check against the PDF 408-409 images: 50x10/70x6 x3, 60-85 x3, the 5-10 kg strip, \"no more than 6 repetitions\", both pyramids, 2-6 min, 4 to 10, 2.5-5 kg, the \"rarely used by serious athletes\" quote and item 7 (PDF 413). | (1) Set `confidence: supported`. (2) Rewrite as \"Inference: step sets can find a working load for a fixed repetition target\" and \"Plateau sets are for novices and transition phases (PDF p. 408). Inference: not for a peaking block.\" |\n| framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md | **FIX** | The contested reason is correct but incomplete, and it leans on the weaker evidence. The same paragraph that sets \"Characteristically, the minimum load used is one's 5RM\" (PDF 409 / printed 398, image) also prescribes extensive clustering \"with one's **4-6RM**\" (a 6RM is lighter than a 5RM) and a stripping variant where \"the load is decreased by 10-20%\" after each set (PDF 409-410). Both fall below the stated minimum in the text itself, without any %1RM conversion. The entry's 75-90% argument depends on reading the pyramid's \"6 x 85%\" as a 6RM, an inference (correctly labelled). All other values check against the PDF 409-410 images: 30-40% / 5-6 x 20-30 / 1-2 min; 50-60% / 3-6 sets to failure / 10-90 s; \">180 beats/min\"; circuits 1-3 min; \"75% of (220-Age)\". The Ch5 comparison (20-40 reps at ≤30% against 3-8 reps at >70%) checks at PDF 315 / printed 304. Baseline lines 119 and 182 are correct. | In \"Why `contested`\", lead with the direct text: \"The same paragraph sets the minimum at 'one's 5RM' (PDF p. 409) but prescribes extensive clustering 'with one's 4-6RM' and a stripping form that cuts the load 'by 10-20%' each set (PDF pp. 409-410), both below that minimum; intensive clustering then gives '75-90% of 1RM' (PDF p. 410).\" Keep the existing pyramid-based Inference after it as supporting reasoning. |\n| framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md | **FIX** (minor) | (1) \"When to apply — When session time or space is short and several movements can be loaded by the same bar\". The authors say only \"Instead of doing three separate exercises at three different stations, one can do all of them in a single repetition or at a single station\" (PDF 410). Time and space are the writer's addition and are unlabelled. (2) The superscript is left \"Unclear as printed ... Inference: the superscript may be a set count\". The book settles this itself: the 6.6.2 convention writes 80 kg x 6 done twice as \"6(80)²\", with the superscript as the set count, and gives the hybrid example \"100 / 2+3+1\" (PDF 336 / printed 325). The PDF 411 image shows 80³ over 3+5+2, so the notation means 3 sets, and the book's \"for a set of 3 repetitions\" is a slip in its own wording. All other content checks against the PDF 410-411 images, including both example lists and the two guidelines. | (1) Prefix \"Inference:\" to the time/space clause, or replace it with \"When a circuit is to be condensed into one station (PDF p. 410).\" (2) Replace the unclear note with: \"By the 6.6.2 convention (PDF p. 336 / printed p. 325, where '6(80)²' means two sets of 6 at 80 kg) the superscript is the number of sets, so 80³ over 3+5+2 means three sets of the 10-repetition hybrid; the text's 'for a set of 3 repetitions' conflicts with its own convention.\" |\n| heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md | **FIX** (minor) | \"When to apply\": \"The random variant when a long block of one exercise has stalled.\" The authors give the random variant a preventive purpose, \"to minimise the possibility of early accommodation to a predictable regime\" (PDF 410 / printed 399, image), not a remedy for a stall. The sentence sits outside the preceding \"Inference:\" sentence. All quotes check against the PDF 410 image, including \"2-4 segments\", \"3-7 repetitions\", Twenty-Ones, Rigert's three segments of 1-3 reps, and \"Frequently ... full clean pull\". | Replace with: \"The random variant from the start of a block, to limit early accommodation (the authors' stated purpose, PDF p. 410).\" If the stall use is wanted, write it as \"Inference: ...\". |\n| framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md | **FIX** | (1) Scope. The entry says \"The heavy-light set and load release rest on exactly the effect those studies dispute.\" The 3.4.2 Western studies test \"the speed of an unloaded movement ... directly after the same movement was executed with a weight\" (PDF 175 / printed 164, image). That is a preceding loaded effort followed by a lighter one, which is the heavy-light set. Load release and pneumatics drop the load inside one repetition. The authors give a different mechanism for them (\"the contrast in stimulus ... produces a rapid acceleration\", PDF 411), and the cited studies did not test it. (2) The evidence is reported one-sided. The same page, PDF 175, reports the Russian side: \"Dynamic work with heavy weights (i.e. high tension with relatively few repetitions) also elicits a positive after-effect in the central nervous system ... and an improvement in speed and strength\" (Portnov 1955 and others), and \"intense preliminary strength tension improves results in jumping exercises ... in shotputting ... and in rowing\". \"When NOT to apply\" says only \"the book's own cited Western studies found none in several tasks\". That is the one-sided reading the Ch1 lesson forbids. All method definitions and the 20% / 10-20 s / 3-5 rep figures check against the PDF 411 image, and the Ch5 shock-method link (contrast methods listed, \"unsuitable for novices\") checks. | (1) In \"Why `contested`\", change \"The heavy-light set and load release rest on\" to \"The heavy-light set rests on\". Add: \"Load release and pneumatics unload within a single repetition; the cited studies did not test that, so the conflict does not reach them directly.\" (2) Add after the Western list: \"The same page reports Russian findings in favour: heavy dynamic work 'elicits a positive after-effect ... and an improvement in speed and strength', and intense preliminary strength tension improves jumping, shot-put and rowing results (PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164).\" In \"When NOT to apply\", change the first sentence to: \"Do not expect a reliable acute boost: the book reports Western studies that found none and Russian studies that found one (PDF p. 175).\" |\n| framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations.md | **GO** | None found. | checked: the \"Miscellaneous Methods\" heading heads PDF 412 (image), followed by the frame quote (\"of minimal significance\", \"shock the body ... out of any prevailing state of habituation or stagnation\"); every method definition on PDF 412-413 (images), including \"30-25-20-15-10\", \"10-12 sets\", \"4-5 sets\", \"10RM, with 10 second rest intervals\" and the functional-priority order; Continuous Tension Sets at PDF 410 (image); the PHA quotes and \"Setchinov\" at PDF 412; the 5.6 Setchinov entry does use the effect for restoration between stations; baseline lines 41 and 109 quoted and tagged correctly. Every Inference is labelled. |\n| heuristic--overcoming-sticking-points-repertoire-of-27-strategies.md | **GO** | None found. | checked: PDF 413-414 images. Items 1-18 run on PDF 413 into 414, then 20-28. There is no item 19, so the list holds 27. The entry's six groups contain every one of the 27 numbers exactly once (1,2,3,7 / 4,6,8 / 5,13 / 9,11,20,21,26,27,28 / 10,15,16,17,18,25 / 12,14,22,23,24). The entry paraphrases with short quotes and does not reprint the list. Checked \"80 - 92.5 - 97.5 - 102.5kg\", \"60 - 80 - 100kg\", \"0.5 - 1.25kg\", the item 12 competition caveat, the item 13 rationale, and the opening quote including the sic \"one of single most\". Baseline lines 30, 61 and 142 quoted and tagged correctly. The Ch5 overtraining caution is cited to its own entry and labelled as not from this list. |\n\nCounts: **GO 3, FIX 7, NO-GO 0.**\n\n## Duplicates (check 5)\n\nNone. Grepped the vault library and the staging library for: cluster, heavy-light, rest-pause, pyramid, twenty-one / segmental, hybrid, sticking point, peripheral heart / PHA, drop set / stripping, superset, pre-exhaust, superslow. The relevant hits cover different angles and are already linked from these entries:\n\n- circuit layouts (superset / stripping circuits)\n- parachute contrast release\n- APRE and DeLorme pyramids\n- Setchinov station sequencing\n- Zatsiorsky's joint-angle \"sticking point\"\n- the Ch3 interval-style energy cost\n\nThe existing CAT entry already covers Compensatory Deceleration and Compensatory Action Training from PDF 411, so leaving CAT out of this batch is correct.\n\n## Missing (check 6)\n\n1. **Quasi-isometrics as a discrete \"superslow\" method** (PDF 405 / printed 394). Quasi-isometrics is unavoidable with near-maximal loads, but as a chosen method it means very slow repetitions with moderate or light loads, \"popularised in bodybuilding circles as the superslow method\". No library entry mentions superslow. It could be a short addition to the Fig 7.1/7.2 entry, which already lists Quasi-isometrics as a box.\n2. **Connective tissue first, plus low-intensity cyclical work in general preparation** (PDF 405 / printed 394). \"Great emphasis should be placed on increasing the mass and strength of the connective tissues ... particularly during the early stages of training and transition phases\", and low-intensity prolonged cyclical activity is \"an essential part of the general preparatory phase of all training\", also useful for restoration. This is a programming rule, not a method, and no entry in this batch captures it. Check `principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue` for overlap before writing a new entry.\n3. **Heavy full-range resistance as active stretching** (PDF 405). \"Several of the other training methods with heavy resistance used over a full range of movement constitute very effective means of active stretching.\" This could be one line in the chart entry or a link from a flexibility entry.\n4. **Concluding scope statement** (PDF 414 / printed 403). The summary covers \"the major methods which are most frequently used in the training of high-level athletes\", and leaves out magazine methods that \"differ only in name and a few minor details\". This is useful context for the confidence of the whole 7.1 catalogue: the authors present it as frequently used practice. It could go in `_book.md` or the chart entry.\n5. **Chapter opening on knowledge sources** (PDF 404 / printed 393). It lists five sources (weightlifting and powerlifting, bodybuilding, supplementary resistance training, physical therapy, scientific research) and says science only recently began testing Iron Game practice. This explains why most of 7.1 rests on practice, not studies. It is low priority, and one sentence in the chart entry would cover it.\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** Required fixes:\n\n1. **Maximal methods:** scope the `contested` reason to the single rest figure, add the \"between sets\" against cluster-context caveat, and drop or correct \"forced reps\" as a higher-risk method with a stated warning.\n2. **Three regimes:** set `confidence: supported`, and label the split-routine/zone sentence as Inference.\n3. **Set structures:** set `confidence: supported`, and label the step-method purpose and \"not for a peaking block\" as Inference.\n4. **Interval and cluster:** add the direct same-paragraph contradictions (4-6RM; the 10-20% strip) to the `contested` reason.\n5. **Hybrid:** label the time/space clause, and resolve the superscript with the PDF 336 / printed 325 convention (it is a set count).\n6. **Segmental:** restate the random variant's use as preventive (PDF 410), or label the stall use as Inference.\n7. **Contrast:** scope `contested` to the heavy-light set (not load release or pneumatics), and report the Russian positive findings from the same PDF 175 page in both \"Why `contested`\" and \"When NOT to apply\".\n\nFixes 2 and 3 apply the confidence rule stated above. If Eric prefers to keep catalogue entries at `untested`, apply that consistently to the `supported` Table 1.1 entry as well, rather than leaving the folder split.\n","numLines":228,"startLine":1,"totalLines":228}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b03e2b56-215c-4837-8675-c5628d007a2b","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
