{"parentUuid":"f22a62bc-8d60-43b8-931d-256a60eeabd4","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aa8a0297ea9df7045","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffrkGmD3Efds28qbQeoo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1214,"cache_read_input_tokens":142551,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1214,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":9814,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffrkEAgzwscu7x2LZ2FX","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"13e000e0-05a5-4a57-a181-0c11ea176182","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:30:32.072Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"13e000e0-05a5-4a57-a181-0c11ea176182","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aa8a0297ea9df7045","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffrkGmD3Efds28qbQeoo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RtB3r9gVUkarBQwKQ5W3Dj","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch7a_opus_round2.md","content":"# Review round 2: Supertraining Chapter 7 lead-in and 7.1 (Ch7a), 10 entries, Opus independent reviewer\n\nDate: 2026-10-03. Scope: the 10 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch7a.txt`. Inputs: round-1 review `staging/review_supertraining_ch7a_opus.md`, fix log `staging/fixes_supertraining_ch7a_applied.md`, standards `docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md`. Source: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx = printed + 11.\n\nHow checked: full text layer of PDF 175, 335, 336 and 404-414 (PyMuPDF). Page images rendered at 200 dpi to `staging/_renders/r2_7a_p{175,335,336,404,411,412,413,414}.png`, plus crops `r2_7a_p411_top.png` (the 80³ notation, 3x) and `r2_7a_p175_mid.png` (the 3.4.2 Western and Russian paragraphs, 2.5x). Opened: PDF 336 full page, PDF 411 top crop, PDF 175 crop. Every fixed sentence in the 8 edited files was read in full against the page, not against the fix log.\n\n## Targeted checks\n\n**Superscript notation (PDF 335-336).** Confirmed from the PDF 336 image. The foot of PDF 335 lists \"Exercise A : 60 x 10, 70 x 8, 80 x 6, 80 x 6\"; PDF 336 opens with the same work written \"10(60)¹, 8(70)¹, 6(80)²\" and as fractions 60/10 .1, 70/8 .1, 80/6 .2. The superscript (or trailing factor) is the set count. The PDF 411 crop shows \"80³\" over \"3+5+2\" with the text \"for a bar loaded to 80kg, for a set of 3 repetitions\". The hybrid entry's reading (three sets of the 10-repetition hybrid; the book's own wording conflicts with its convention) is correct, and citing PDF 335-336 rather than 336 alone is right. One detail the entry does not claim and does not need: the 6.6.2 hybrid example \"Exercise B: 100 / 2+3+1\" carries no superscript, so the convention for hybrids is shown on PDF 336 only by analogy with Exercise A. The entry's inference holds.\n\n**PDF 175 Western and Russian findings.** Confirmed from the crop. Western: \"a statistically significant increase in the speed of an unloaded movement was found directly after the same movement was executed with a weight (Murray, 1959; Van Huss et al, 1962)\"; no effect in Nofsinger 1963 and \"Nelson & Lamber, 1965\"; no vertical jump increase (Stockholm & Nelson, 1965); shot-put worse (Bischke & Morehouse, 1950). Russian: both verbatim quotes in the contrast entry match the image word for word, citation lists included. The fixer's disagreement with round 1 is justified: round 1's proposed \"Western studies that found none\" would have dropped Murray and Van Huss. The entry's \"the Western studies the book cites are split (two found a faster unloaded movement, others found none), and the Russian studies it cites found one\" is accurate.\n\n**6RM vs 5RM (interval and cluster entry).** The arithmetic is right (a 6RM is lighter than a 5RM). But the sentence still reads \"prescribes extensive clustering 'with one's 4-6RM' (a 6RM is lighter than a 5RM) and a stripping form ... both of which take the load below that minimum\". The 4RM and 5RM ends of \"4-6RM\" are at or above the 5RM minimum, so only the 6RM end does. The fix log says this was narrowed (Disagreement 1), but the entry text still asserts it of the whole range. One-word fix below.\n\n## Entry verdicts\n\n| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md | **GO** | None found. | checked: the five sources and \"Only in recent years, has science begun to investigate seriously ...\" at PDF 404 / printed 393; superslow, active stretching, connective tissue and cyclical-activity quotes at PDF 405 / printed 394, each word for word; \"rapid reference for the major methods which are most frequently used in the training of high-level athletes\" and the magazine-methods exclusion at PDF 414 / printed 403; the closing Inference is labelled; new links resolve and their stated chapters match (`framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology` is Ch 1.8-1.11 and defines quasi-isometric; `claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates` is Ch 1.6; `principle--loading-volume-...` is Ch 6.9.4); new When NOT line matches PDF 404. Fig 7.1/7.2 content was GO in round 1 and is unchanged. `untested` fits the rule (taxonomy with one unevidenced ordering). |\n| framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md | **GO** | Fix 1 landed in all three parts. | checked: scope sentence opens \"Why `contested`\"; \"As worded, both sentences describe\"; \"optimal recovery intervals between sets\" at PDF 406 / printed 395 (the page says \"This 1RM set with optimal recovery intervals between sets\", a fair reading); the PDF 409 sentence does open the interval section that runs into Cluster Training (10-20 s \"in an extended set\"); When NOT now names injury warnings for eccentrics (\"carries an increased risk of injury\") and cheating (\"if injury is to be avoided\") only, with no warning for forced reps, matching PDF 407. The consistency edit (\"two values that may describe different methods\") matches the scoped reason. `contested` scoped to one rest figure is consistent with the rule. |\n| framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md | **FIX** (minor) | Fix 2 landed (`supported`; \"Inference: split routines and zone training ...\"). New inconsistency exposed by the confidence change: When NOT opens \"Not as sport strength methods. The authors say bodybuilding methods 'are of minimal significance ...' (PDF p. 412)\". On PDF 412 that sentence refers to \"a host of different bodybuilding methods\" introduced under Miscellaneous Methods (\"These are not discussed at length here because they are of minimal significance\"), not to the three regimes on PDF 408. The fix log uses that same quote to keep the miscellaneous entry `untested` while this entry goes to `supported`, so the quote cannot belong to both. The PDF 405 rule also says that if the strength deficit is small \"submaximal bodybuilding methods should be relied upon\" for athletes, so \"Not as sport strength methods\" overstates. | Replace the first two sentences of When NOT with: \"Not as the main method for speed-strength or explosive qualities: the authors send athletes with a large strength deficit to weightlifting and reactive methods, and keep submaximal bodybuilding methods for a small deficit (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394).\" Keep the supersets sentence and the rest unchanged. |\n| framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md | **GO** | Fix 3 landed. | checked: `supported`; \"Inference: step sets can find a working load ...\"; \"Plateau sets are for novices and transition phases (PDF p. 408). Inference: not for a peaking block.\" Plateau \"commonly used during the early stages of training novices or for transitional active rest phases\", stripping \"commonly used for muscle endurance and definition training\" (PDF 408 / printed 397), pyramid values and \"rarely used by serious athletes today\" (PDF 409 / printed 398), item 7 (PDF 413 / printed 402). Keeping the \"rarely used\" caveat alongside `supported` is coherent: the structures are established; the caveat is the authors' preference among them. |\n| framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md | **FIX** (minor) | Fix 4 landed: the direct same-paragraph statements (5RM minimum, 4-6RM, 10-20% strip, 75-90%) lead \"Why `contested`\" and the When NOT list, all matching PDF 409-410 / printed 398-399. Two problems remain. (1) \"both of which take the load below that minimum\" is true of the 6RM end and the 10-20% strip, not of the 4RM-5RM end of \"4-6RM\"; the fix log claims a narrowing that the sentence does not carry. (2) Consistency with the scoped `contested` reasons in the maximal and contrast entries: this entry's reason never says the extensive and intensive interval prescriptions (30-40% / 5-6 x 20-30 / 1-2 min; 50-60% / 3-6 sets to failure / 10-90 s) are not in dispute, so a reader filtering by confidence would read them as contested. | (1) Change \"both of which take the load below that minimum\" to \"both of which can take the load below that minimum\". (2) Open \"Why `contested`\" with: \"The rating applies only to the cluster-set loads; the extensive and intensive interval prescriptions are not in dispute.\" |\n| framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md | **GO** | Fix 5 landed. | checked: the superscript paragraph against the PDF 336 image and the PDF 411 crop (see Targeted checks); \"When a circuit is to be condensed into one station (the authors' use, PDF p. 410)\" matches \"Instead of doing three separate exercises at three different stations, one can do all of them in a single repetition or at a single station\"; the time/space clause is now labelled Inference; Example reads \"written 80³ (three sets by the 6.6.2 convention)\". `untested` fits the rule: examples \"may be devised by the inventive coach\" (PDF 411), no load or rest parameters. |\n| heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md | **GO** | Fix 6 landed. | checked: \"to minimise the possibility of early accommodation to a predictable regime\" (PDF 410 / printed 399); the stall use is gone. \"From the start of a block\" is a reasonable reading of \"early accommodation\", not a new claim. \"2-4 segments\", \"3-7 repetitions\", Twenty-Ones, Rigert's 1-3 reps per segment and \"Frequently ... full clean pull\" re-checked against the text. `untested` is defensible but borderline: progressive segmental has parameters (2-4 segments, 3-7 reps) and Twenty-Ones is \"A popular form\"; no load or rest is given, the random variant is a named novelty, and the entry is built on the Rigert anecdote. I accept the fix log's reasoning; no change required. |\n| framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md | **GO** | Fix 7 landed in both parts. | checked: \"The rating applies to the heavy-light set\"; \"The heavy-light set rests on exactly the effect these studies disagree about\"; load release and pneumatics scoped out (\"unload within a single repetition\"), consistent with PDF 411's separate \"contrast in stimulus\" mechanism; Western and Russian quotes verbatim against the PDF 175 crop; When NOT balanced (see Targeted checks). Heavy-light 20% / 10-20 s / 3-5 reps and the dip/pull-up example re-checked at PDF 411 / printed 400. `contested` scoped to heavy-light is consistent with the rule. |\n| framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations.md | **GO** | Spot check: none found. | checked: frame quotes (\"minimal significance\", \"novel variation to shock the body ... habituation or stagnation\", \"hypertrophy phase\") at PDF 412 / printed 401; strict sets, major-to-minor wording and the functional-priority order, PHA quote and Setchinov, diminishing sets \"usually 100\", \"30-25-20-15-10\", \"10-12 sets\", \"4-5 sets\" (PDF 412); cumulative sets \"10RM, with 10 second rest intervals\" and peak contraction (PDF 413); continuous tension (PDF 410). `untested` fits the rule: the authors frame these as novelties of minimal significance. |\n| heuristic--overcoming-sticking-points-repertoire-of-27-strategies.md | **FIX** (minor) | Spot check: all quotes and numbers check against PDF 413-414 (no item 19, 27 items; \"80 - 92.5 - 97.5 - 102.5kg\", \"60 - 80 - 100kg\", \"0.5 - 1.25kg\", item 12 competition limit, item 13 rest testing, sic \"one of single most\"). One unlabelled writer prescription: When to apply opens \"When a lift has stalled for weeks in a lifter with a training history, as a checklist to pick one or two changes at a time.\" The authors give no duration (\"for weeks\"), no lifter qualification, and no \"one or two changes at a time\" rule; they say only that the list is \"a repertoire of various strategies which may implemented to help one break through these performance barriers\" (PDF 413 / printed 402). | Replace with: \"When a lift has reached a sticking point, as a repertoire to choose from (PDF p. 413). Inference: change one or two things at a time, in a lifter with a training history, so the cause of any change can be seen.\" |\n\nCounts: **GO 7, FIX 3, NO-GO 0.**\n\n## Confidence rule across the 10\n\nRule: established, parameterised practice the authors present with loads, reps, rest or other parameters is `supported`; anecdotes, novelties the authors discount, tip lists and unevidenced orderings are `untested`; `contested` only for a recorded same-question conflict, with the reason scoped.\n\n| Entry | Rating | Consistent? |\n|---|---|---|\n| Chart (Figs 7.1, 7.2) | untested | Yes. Taxonomy; the nervous-system arrow is an unevidenced ordering. |\n| Maximal / supramaximal / circa / submax | contested | Yes. Scoped to the rest between singles. |\n| Three bodybuilding regimes | supported | Yes. Sets, reps, load and rest; \"one of the best known\" and \"popularly used\" (PDF 408). The When NOT quote must not be the \"minimal significance\" frame that keeps the miscellaneous entry `untested` (FIX above). |\n| Set structures | supported | Yes. Parameterised; \"commonly used\" (PDF 408); pyramid values from Fig 1.9 (PDF 409). |\n| Interval and cluster | contested | Yes on the conflict; add the scope sentence (FIX above) so the interval prescriptions are not read as disputed. |\n| Hybrid | untested | Yes. Repetition counts only; the coach's own inventions (PDF 411). |\n| Segmental | untested | Yes, borderline (see row). |\n| Contrast | contested | Yes. Scoped to heavy-light. |\n| Miscellaneous | untested | Yes. Novelties of \"minimal significance\" (PDF 412). |\n| Sticking points | untested | Yes. Tip list, \"Among some of the methods are the following\" (PDF 413). |\n\nThe two upgrades to `supported` are justified by the page. The eight unchanged ratings are consistent with the rule.\n\n## Mechanical checks (all 10 files)\n\n- Six section headers in the vault form `**X** —`: 6 in every file.\n- Em dashes outside the six headers: none.\n- Wrapped wikilinks: none.\n- Review-history or process wording (round, reviewer, fixer, corrected, prior version, NO-GO, \"now reads\"): none.\n- Wikilinks: all 45 distinct targets resolve, in the staging library or the vault (`/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library`), including the two path-qualified Zatsiorsky links.\n\n## Missing\n\nNone new. The five round-1 missing ideas are placed in the chart entry and quoted correctly (PDF 404, 405, 414). The decision not to write a separate entry for low-intensity cyclical work (two sentences, no parameters) is reasonable.\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** All seven round-1 fixes landed and are correct on the page; the fixer's three disagreements with round 1 are justified. Three minor fixes remain:\n\n1. **Three regimes:** replace the \"minimal significance\" opening of When NOT (that PDF 412 sentence belongs to the Miscellaneous Methods frame) with the PDF 405 deficit-based limit given above.\n2. **Interval and cluster:** \"take\" to \"can take\" for the 4-6RM sentence, and add the scope sentence to \"Why `contested`\".\n3. **Sticking points:** label the \"for weeks\", \"training history\" and \"one or two changes at a time\" framing in When to apply as Inference.\n\nThese are wording edits with no source re-check needed; after they are applied the batch can be promoted without another review round.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1214,"cache_read_input_tokens":142551,"output_tokens":6981,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1062},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1214},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":6981,"cache_read_input_tokens":142551,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1214,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1214,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01RtB3r9gVUkarBQwKQ5W3Dj":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch7a_opus_round2.md","content":"# Review round 2: Supertraining Chapter 7 lead-in and 7.1 (Ch7a), 10 entries, Opus independent reviewer\n\nDate: 2026-10-03. Scope: the 10 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch7a.txt`. Inputs: round-1 review `staging/review_supertraining_ch7a_opus.md`, fix log `staging/fixes_supertraining_ch7a_applied.md`, standards `docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md`. Source: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx = printed + 11.\n\nHow checked: full text layer of PDF 175, 335, 336 and 404-414 (PyMuPDF). Page images rendered at 200 dpi to `staging/_renders/r2_7a_p{175,335,336,404,411,412,413,414}.png`, plus crops `r2_7a_p411_top.png` (the 80³ notation, 3x) and `r2_7a_p175_mid.png` (the 3.4.2 Western and Russian paragraphs, 2.5x). Opened: PDF 336 full page, PDF 411 top crop, PDF 175 crop. Every fixed sentence in the 8 edited files was read in full against the page, not against the fix log.\n\n## Targeted checks\n\n**Superscript notation (PDF 335-336).** Confirmed from the PDF 336 image. The foot of PDF 335 lists \"Exercise A : 60 x 10, 70 x 8, 80 x 6, 80 x 6\"; PDF 336 opens with the same work written \"10(60)¹, 8(70)¹, 6(80)²\" and as fractions 60/10 .1, 70/8 .1, 80/6 .2. The superscript (or trailing factor) is the set count. The PDF 411 crop shows \"80³\" over \"3+5+2\" with the text \"for a bar loaded to 80kg, for a set of 3 repetitions\". The hybrid entry's reading (three sets of the 10-repetition hybrid; the book's own wording conflicts with its convention) is correct, and citing PDF 335-336 rather than 336 alone is right. One detail the entry does not claim and does not need: the 6.6.2 hybrid example \"Exercise B: 100 / 2+3+1\" carries no superscript, so the convention for hybrids is shown on PDF 336 only by analogy with Exercise A. The entry's inference holds.\n\n**PDF 175 Western and Russian findings.** Confirmed from the crop. Western: \"a statistically significant increase in the speed of an unloaded movement was found directly after the same movement was executed with a weight (Murray, 1959; Van Huss et al, 1962)\"; no effect in Nofsinger 1963 and \"Nelson & Lamber, 1965\"; no vertical jump increase (Stockholm & Nelson, 1965); shot-put worse (Bischke & Morehouse, 1950). Russian: both verbatim quotes in the contrast entry match the image word for word, citation lists included. The fixer's disagreement with round 1 is justified: round 1's proposed \"Western studies that found none\" would have dropped Murray and Van Huss. The entry's \"the Western studies the book cites are split (two found a faster unloaded movement, others found none), and the Russian studies it cites found one\" is accurate.\n\n**6RM vs 5RM (interval and cluster entry).** The arithmetic is right (a 6RM is lighter than a 5RM). But the sentence still reads \"prescribes extensive clustering 'with one's 4-6RM' (a 6RM is lighter than a 5RM) and a stripping form ... both of which take the load below that minimum\". The 4RM and 5RM ends of \"4-6RM\" are at or above the 5RM minimum, so only the 6RM end does. The fix log says this was narrowed (Disagreement 1), but the entry text still asserts it of the whole range. One-word fix below.\n\n## Entry verdicts\n\n| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md | **GO** | None found. | checked: the five sources and \"Only in recent years, has science begun to investigate seriously ...\" at PDF 404 / printed 393; superslow, active stretching, connective tissue and cyclical-activity quotes at PDF 405 / printed 394, each word for word; \"rapid reference for the major methods which are most frequently used in the training of high-level athletes\" and the magazine-methods exclusion at PDF 414 / printed 403; the closing Inference is labelled; new links resolve and their stated chapters match (`framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology` is Ch 1.8-1.11 and defines quasi-isometric; `claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates` is Ch 1.6; `principle--loading-volume-...` is Ch 6.9.4); new When NOT line matches PDF 404. Fig 7.1/7.2 content was GO in round 1 and is unchanged. `untested` fits the rule (taxonomy with one unevidenced ordering). |\n| framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md | **GO** | Fix 1 landed in all three parts. | checked: scope sentence opens \"Why `contested`\"; \"As worded, both sentences describe\"; \"optimal recovery intervals between sets\" at PDF 406 / printed 395 (the page says \"This 1RM set with optimal recovery intervals between sets\", a fair reading); the PDF 409 sentence does open the interval section that runs into Cluster Training (10-20 s \"in an extended set\"); When NOT now names injury warnings for eccentrics (\"carries an increased risk of injury\") and cheating (\"if injury is to be avoided\") only, with no warning for forced reps, matching PDF 407. The consistency edit (\"two values that may describe different methods\") matches the scoped reason. `contested` scoped to one rest figure is consistent with the rule. |\n| framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md | **FIX** (minor) | Fix 2 landed (`supported`; \"Inference: split routines and zone training ...\"). New inconsistency exposed by the confidence change: When NOT opens \"Not as sport strength methods. The authors say bodybuilding methods 'are of minimal significance ...' (PDF p. 412)\". On PDF 412 that sentence refers to \"a host of different bodybuilding methods\" introduced under Miscellaneous Methods (\"These are not discussed at length here because they are of minimal significance\"), not to the three regimes on PDF 408. The fix log uses that same quote to keep the miscellaneous entry `untested` while this entry goes to `supported`, so the quote cannot belong to both. The PDF 405 rule also says that if the strength deficit is small \"submaximal bodybuilding methods should be relied upon\" for athletes, so \"Not as sport strength methods\" overstates. | Replace the first two sentences of When NOT with: \"Not as the main method for speed-strength or explosive qualities: the authors send athletes with a large strength deficit to weightlifting and reactive methods, and keep submaximal bodybuilding methods for a small deficit (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394).\" Keep the supersets sentence and the rest unchanged. |\n| framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md | **GO** | Fix 3 landed. | checked: `supported`; \"Inference: step sets can find a working load ...\"; \"Plateau sets are for novices and transition phases (PDF p. 408). Inference: not for a peaking block.\" Plateau \"commonly used during the early stages of training novices or for transitional active rest phases\", stripping \"commonly used for muscle endurance and definition training\" (PDF 408 / printed 397), pyramid values and \"rarely used by serious athletes today\" (PDF 409 / printed 398), item 7 (PDF 413 / printed 402). Keeping the \"rarely used\" caveat alongside `supported` is coherent: the structures are established; the caveat is the authors' preference among them. |\n| framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md | **FIX** (minor) | Fix 4 landed: the direct same-paragraph statements (5RM minimum, 4-6RM, 10-20% strip, 75-90%) lead \"Why `contested`\" and the When NOT list, all matching PDF 409-410 / printed 398-399. Two problems remain. (1) \"both of which take the load below that minimum\" is true of the 6RM end and the 10-20% strip, not of the 4RM-5RM end of \"4-6RM\"; the fix log claims a narrowing that the sentence does not carry. (2) Consistency with the scoped `contested` reasons in the maximal and contrast entries: this entry's reason never says the extensive and intensive interval prescriptions (30-40% / 5-6 x 20-30 / 1-2 min; 50-60% / 3-6 sets to failure / 10-90 s) are not in dispute, so a reader filtering by confidence would read them as contested. | (1) Change \"both of which take the load below that minimum\" to \"both of which can take the load below that minimum\". (2) Open \"Why `contested`\" with: \"The rating applies only to the cluster-set loads; the extensive and intensive interval prescriptions are not in dispute.\" |\n| framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md | **GO** | Fix 5 landed. | checked: the superscript paragraph against the PDF 336 image and the PDF 411 crop (see Targeted checks); \"When a circuit is to be condensed into one station (the authors' use, PDF p. 410)\" matches \"Instead of doing three separate exercises at three different stations, one can do all of them in a single repetition or at a single station\"; the time/space clause is now labelled Inference; Example reads \"written 80³ (three sets by the 6.6.2 convention)\". `untested` fits the rule: examples \"may be devised by the inventive coach\" (PDF 411), no load or rest parameters. |\n| heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md | **GO** | Fix 6 landed. | checked: \"to minimise the possibility of early accommodation to a predictable regime\" (PDF 410 / printed 399); the stall use is gone. \"From the start of a block\" is a reasonable reading of \"early accommodation\", not a new claim. \"2-4 segments\", \"3-7 repetitions\", Twenty-Ones, Rigert's 1-3 reps per segment and \"Frequently ... full clean pull\" re-checked against the text. `untested` is defensible but borderline: progressive segmental has parameters (2-4 segments, 3-7 reps) and Twenty-Ones is \"A popular form\"; no load or rest is given, the random variant is a named novelty, and the entry is built on the Rigert anecdote. I accept the fix log's reasoning; no change required. |\n| framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md | **GO** | Fix 7 landed in both parts. | checked: \"The rating applies to the heavy-light set\"; \"The heavy-light set rests on exactly the effect these studies disagree about\"; load release and pneumatics scoped out (\"unload within a single repetition\"), consistent with PDF 411's separate \"contrast in stimulus\" mechanism; Western and Russian quotes verbatim against the PDF 175 crop; When NOT balanced (see Targeted checks). Heavy-light 20% / 10-20 s / 3-5 reps and the dip/pull-up example re-checked at PDF 411 / printed 400. `contested` scoped to heavy-light is consistent with the rule. |\n| framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations.md | **GO** | Spot check: none found. | checked: frame quotes (\"minimal significance\", \"novel variation to shock the body ... habituation or stagnation\", \"hypertrophy phase\") at PDF 412 / printed 401; strict sets, major-to-minor wording and the functional-priority order, PHA quote and Setchinov, diminishing sets \"usually 100\", \"30-25-20-15-10\", \"10-12 sets\", \"4-5 sets\" (PDF 412); cumulative sets \"10RM, with 10 second rest intervals\" and peak contraction (PDF 413); continuous tension (PDF 410). `untested` fits the rule: the authors frame these as novelties of minimal significance. |\n| heuristic--overcoming-sticking-points-repertoire-of-27-strategies.md | **FIX** (minor) | Spot check: all quotes and numbers check against PDF 413-414 (no item 19, 27 items; \"80 - 92.5 - 97.5 - 102.5kg\", \"60 - 80 - 100kg\", \"0.5 - 1.25kg\", item 12 competition limit, item 13 rest testing, sic \"one of single most\"). One unlabelled writer prescription: When to apply opens \"When a lift has stalled for weeks in a lifter with a training history, as a checklist to pick one or two changes at a time.\" The authors give no duration (\"for weeks\"), no lifter qualification, and no \"one or two changes at a time\" rule; they say only that the list is \"a repertoire of various strategies which may implemented to help one break through these performance barriers\" (PDF 413 / printed 402). | Replace with: \"When a lift has reached a sticking point, as a repertoire to choose from (PDF p. 413). Inference: change one or two things at a time, in a lifter with a training history, so the cause of any change can be seen.\" |\n\nCounts: **GO 7, FIX 3, NO-GO 0.**\n\n## Confidence rule across the 10\n\nRule: established, parameterised practice the authors present with loads, reps, rest or other parameters is `supported`; anecdotes, novelties the authors discount, tip lists and unevidenced orderings are `untested`; `contested` only for a recorded same-question conflict, with the reason scoped.\n\n| Entry | Rating | Consistent? |\n|---|---|---|\n| Chart (Figs 7.1, 7.2) | untested | Yes. Taxonomy; the nervous-system arrow is an unevidenced ordering. |\n| Maximal / supramaximal / circa / submax | contested | Yes. Scoped to the rest between singles. |\n| Three bodybuilding regimes | supported | Yes. Sets, reps, load and rest; \"one of the best known\" and \"popularly used\" (PDF 408). The When NOT quote must not be the \"minimal significance\" frame that keeps the miscellaneous entry `untested` (FIX above). |\n| Set structures | supported | Yes. Parameterised; \"commonly used\" (PDF 408); pyramid values from Fig 1.9 (PDF 409). |\n| Interval and cluster | contested | Yes on the conflict; add the scope sentence (FIX above) so the interval prescriptions are not read as disputed. |\n| Hybrid | untested | Yes. Repetition counts only; the coach's own inventions (PDF 411). |\n| Segmental | untested | Yes, borderline (see row). |\n| Contrast | contested | Yes. Scoped to heavy-light. |\n| Miscellaneous | untested | Yes. Novelties of \"minimal significance\" (PDF 412). |\n| Sticking points | untested | Yes. Tip list, \"Among some of the methods are the following\" (PDF 413). |\n\nThe two upgrades to `supported` are justified by the page. The eight unchanged ratings are consistent with the rule.\n\n## Mechanical checks (all 10 files)\n\n- Six section headers in the vault form `**X** —`: 6 in every file.\n- Em dashes outside the six headers: none.\n- Wrapped wikilinks: none.\n- Review-history or process wording (round, reviewer, fixer, corrected, prior version, NO-GO, \"now reads\"): none.\n- Wikilinks: all 45 distinct targets resolve, in the staging library or the vault (`/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library`), including the two path-qualified Zatsiorsky links.\n\n## Missing\n\nNone new. The five round-1 missing ideas are placed in the chart entry and quoted correctly (PDF 404, 405, 414). The decision not to write a separate entry for low-intensity cyclical work (two sentences, no parameters) is reasonable.\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** All seven round-1 fixes landed and are correct on the page; the fixer's three disagreements with round 1 are justified. Three minor fixes remain:\n\n1. **Three regimes:** replace the \"minimal significance\" opening of When NOT (that PDF 412 sentence belongs to the Miscellaneous Methods frame) with the PDF 405 deficit-based limit given above.\n2. **Interval and cluster:** \"take\" to \"can take\" for the 4-6RM sentence, and add the scope sentence to \"Why `contested`\".\n3. **Sticking points:** label the \"for weeks\", \"training history\" and \"one or two changes at a time\" framing in When to apply as Inference.\n\nThese are wording edits with no source re-check needed; after they are applied the batch can be promoted without another review round.\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffrkEAgzwscu7x2LZ2FX","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"b6c4c818-3030-4dde-a1f1-9583bc00cacf","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:31:20.711Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"b6c4c818-3030-4dde-a1f1-9583bc00cacf","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ef194c20-d5f8-4d01-bce0-254fc908bea5","agentId":"aa8a0297ea9df7045","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RtB3r9gVUkarBQwKQ5W3Dj","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch7a_opus_round2.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"915a064a-fa92-4a75-81db-8d31999c37f7","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:31:20.845Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch7a_opus_round2.md","content":"# Review round 2: Supertraining Chapter 7 lead-in and 7.1 (Ch7a), 10 entries, Opus independent reviewer\n\nDate: 2026-10-03. Scope: the 10 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch7a.txt`. Inputs: round-1 review `staging/review_supertraining_ch7a_opus.md`, fix log `staging/fixes_supertraining_ch7a_applied.md`, standards `docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md`. Source: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx = printed + 11.\n\nHow checked: full text layer of PDF 175, 335, 336 and 404-414 (PyMuPDF). Page images rendered at 200 dpi to `staging/_renders/r2_7a_p{175,335,336,404,411,412,413,414}.png`, plus crops `r2_7a_p411_top.png` (the 80³ notation, 3x) and `r2_7a_p175_mid.png` (the 3.4.2 Western and Russian paragraphs, 2.5x). Opened: PDF 336 full page, PDF 411 top crop, PDF 175 crop. Every fixed sentence in the 8 edited files was read in full against the page, not against the fix log.\n\n## Targeted checks\n\n**Superscript notation (PDF 335-336).** Confirmed from the PDF 336 image. The foot of PDF 335 lists \"Exercise A : 60 x 10, 70 x 8, 80 x 6, 80 x 6\"; PDF 336 opens with the same work written \"10(60)¹, 8(70)¹, 6(80)²\" and as fractions 60/10 .1, 70/8 .1, 80/6 .2. The superscript (or trailing factor) is the set count. The PDF 411 crop shows \"80³\" over \"3+5+2\" with the text \"for a bar loaded to 80kg, for a set of 3 repetitions\". The hybrid entry's reading (three sets of the 10-repetition hybrid; the book's own wording conflicts with its convention) is correct, and citing PDF 335-336 rather than 336 alone is right. One detail the entry does not claim and does not need: the 6.6.2 hybrid example \"Exercise B: 100 / 2+3+1\" carries no superscript, so the convention for hybrids is shown on PDF 336 only by analogy with Exercise A. The entry's inference holds.\n\n**PDF 175 Western and Russian findings.** Confirmed from the crop. Western: \"a statistically significant increase in the speed of an unloaded movement was found directly after the same movement was executed with a weight (Murray, 1959; Van Huss et al, 1962)\"; no effect in Nofsinger 1963 and \"Nelson & Lamber, 1965\"; no vertical jump increase (Stockholm & Nelson, 1965); shot-put worse (Bischke & Morehouse, 1950). Russian: both verbatim quotes in the contrast entry match the image word for word, citation lists included. The fixer's disagreement with round 1 is justified: round 1's proposed \"Western studies that found none\" would have dropped Murray and Van Huss. The entry's \"the Western studies the book cites are split (two found a faster unloaded movement, others found none), and the Russian studies it cites found one\" is accurate.\n\n**6RM vs 5RM (interval and cluster entry).** The arithmetic is right (a 6RM is lighter than a 5RM). But the sentence still reads \"prescribes extensive clustering 'with one's 4-6RM' (a 6RM is lighter than a 5RM) and a stripping form ... both of which take the load below that minimum\". The 4RM and 5RM ends of \"4-6RM\" are at or above the 5RM minimum, so only the 6RM end does. The fix log says this was narrowed (Disagreement 1), but the entry text still asserts it of the whole range. One-word fix below.\n\n## Entry verdicts\n\n| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md | **GO** | None found. | checked: the five sources and \"Only in recent years, has science begun to investigate seriously ...\" at PDF 404 / printed 393; superslow, active stretching, connective tissue and cyclical-activity quotes at PDF 405 / printed 394, each word for word; \"rapid reference for the major methods which are most frequently used in the training of high-level athletes\" and the magazine-methods exclusion at PDF 414 / printed 403; the closing Inference is labelled; new links resolve and their stated chapters match (`framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology` is Ch 1.8-1.11 and defines quasi-isometric; `claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates` is Ch 1.6; `principle--loading-volume-...` is Ch 6.9.4); new When NOT line matches PDF 404. Fig 7.1/7.2 content was GO in round 1 and is unchanged. `untested` fits the rule (taxonomy with one unevidenced ordering). |\n| framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md | **GO** | Fix 1 landed in all three parts. | checked: scope sentence opens \"Why `contested`\"; \"As worded, both sentences describe\"; \"optimal recovery intervals between sets\" at PDF 406 / printed 395 (the page says \"This 1RM set with optimal recovery intervals between sets\", a fair reading); the PDF 409 sentence does open the interval section that runs into Cluster Training (10-20 s \"in an extended set\"); When NOT now names injury warnings for eccentrics (\"carries an increased risk of injury\") and cheating (\"if injury is to be avoided\") only, with no warning for forced reps, matching PDF 407. The consistency edit (\"two values that may describe different methods\") matches the scoped reason. `contested` scoped to one rest figure is consistent with the rule. |\n| framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md | **FIX** (minor) | Fix 2 landed (`supported`; \"Inference: split routines and zone training ...\"). New inconsistency exposed by the confidence change: When NOT opens \"Not as sport strength methods. The authors say bodybuilding methods 'are of minimal significance ...' (PDF p. 412)\". On PDF 412 that sentence refers to \"a host of different bodybuilding methods\" introduced under Miscellaneous Methods (\"These are not discussed at length here because they are of minimal significance\"), not to the three regimes on PDF 408. The fix log uses that same quote to keep the miscellaneous entry `untested` while this entry goes to `supported`, so the quote cannot belong to both. The PDF 405 rule also says that if the strength deficit is small \"submaximal bodybuilding methods should be relied upon\" for athletes, so \"Not as sport strength methods\" overstates. | Replace the first two sentences of When NOT with: \"Not as the main method for speed-strength or explosive qualities: the authors send athletes with a large strength deficit to weightlifting and reactive methods, and keep submaximal bodybuilding methods for a small deficit (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394).\" Keep the supersets sentence and the rest unchanged. |\n| framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md | **GO** | Fix 3 landed. | checked: `supported`; \"Inference: step sets can find a working load ...\"; \"Plateau sets are for novices and transition phases (PDF p. 408). Inference: not for a peaking block.\" Plateau \"commonly used during the early stages of training novices or for transitional active rest phases\", stripping \"commonly used for muscle endurance and definition training\" (PDF 408 / printed 397), pyramid values and \"rarely used by serious athletes today\" (PDF 409 / printed 398), item 7 (PDF 413 / printed 402). Keeping the \"rarely used\" caveat alongside `supported` is coherent: the structures are established; the caveat is the authors' preference among them. |\n| framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md | **FIX** (minor) | Fix 4 landed: the direct same-paragraph statements (5RM minimum, 4-6RM, 10-20% strip, 75-90%) lead \"Why `contested`\" and the When NOT list, all matching PDF 409-410 / printed 398-399. Two problems remain. (1) \"both of which take the load below that minimum\" is true of the 6RM end and the 10-20% strip, not of the 4RM-5RM end of \"4-6RM\"; the fix log claims a narrowing that the sentence does not carry. (2) Consistency with the scoped `contested` reasons in the maximal and contrast entries: this entry's reason never says the extensive and intensive interval prescriptions (30-40% / 5-6 x 20-30 / 1-2 min; 50-60% / 3-6 sets to failure / 10-90 s) are not in dispute, so a reader filtering by confidence would read them as contested. | (1) Change \"both of which take the load below that minimum\" to \"both of which can take the load below that minimum\". (2) Open \"Why `contested`\" with: \"The rating applies only to the cluster-set loads; the extensive and intensive interval prescriptions are not in dispute.\" |\n| framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md | **GO** | Fix 5 landed. | checked: the superscript paragraph against the PDF 336 image and the PDF 411 crop (see Targeted checks); \"When a circuit is to be condensed into one station (the authors' use, PDF p. 410)\" matches \"Instead of doing three separate exercises at three different stations, one can do all of them in a single repetition or at a single station\"; the time/space clause is now labelled Inference; Example reads \"written 80³ (three sets by the 6.6.2 convention)\". `untested` fits the rule: examples \"may be devised by the inventive coach\" (PDF 411), no load or rest parameters. |\n| heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md | **GO** | Fix 6 landed. | checked: \"to minimise the possibility of early accommodation to a predictable regime\" (PDF 410 / printed 399); the stall use is gone. \"From the start of a block\" is a reasonable reading of \"early accommodation\", not a new claim. \"2-4 segments\", \"3-7 repetitions\", Twenty-Ones, Rigert's 1-3 reps per segment and \"Frequently ... full clean pull\" re-checked against the text. `untested` is defensible but borderline: progressive segmental has parameters (2-4 segments, 3-7 reps) and Twenty-Ones is \"A popular form\"; no load or rest is given, the random variant is a named novelty, and the entry is built on the Rigert anecdote. I accept the fix log's reasoning; no change required. |\n| framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md | **GO** | Fix 7 landed in both parts. | checked: \"The rating applies to the heavy-light set\"; \"The heavy-light set rests on exactly the effect these studies disagree about\"; load release and pneumatics scoped out (\"unload within a single repetition\"), consistent with PDF 411's separate \"contrast in stimulus\" mechanism; Western and Russian quotes verbatim against the PDF 175 crop; When NOT balanced (see Targeted checks). Heavy-light 20% / 10-20 s / 3-5 reps and the dip/pull-up example re-checked at PDF 411 / printed 400. `contested` scoped to heavy-light is consistent with the rule. |\n| framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations.md | **GO** | Spot check: none found. | checked: frame quotes (\"minimal significance\", \"novel variation to shock the body ... habituation or stagnation\", \"hypertrophy phase\") at PDF 412 / printed 401; strict sets, major-to-minor wording and the functional-priority order, PHA quote and Setchinov, diminishing sets \"usually 100\", \"30-25-20-15-10\", \"10-12 sets\", \"4-5 sets\" (PDF 412); cumulative sets \"10RM, with 10 second rest intervals\" and peak contraction (PDF 413); continuous tension (PDF 410). `untested` fits the rule: the authors frame these as novelties of minimal significance. |\n| heuristic--overcoming-sticking-points-repertoire-of-27-strategies.md | **FIX** (minor) | Spot check: all quotes and numbers check against PDF 413-414 (no item 19, 27 items; \"80 - 92.5 - 97.5 - 102.5kg\", \"60 - 80 - 100kg\", \"0.5 - 1.25kg\", item 12 competition limit, item 13 rest testing, sic \"one of single most\"). One unlabelled writer prescription: When to apply opens \"When a lift has stalled for weeks in a lifter with a training history, as a checklist to pick one or two changes at a time.\" The authors give no duration (\"for weeks\"), no lifter qualification, and no \"one or two changes at a time\" rule; they say only that the list is \"a repertoire of various strategies which may implemented to help one break through these performance barriers\" (PDF 413 / printed 402). | Replace with: \"When a lift has reached a sticking point, as a repertoire to choose from (PDF p. 413). Inference: change one or two things at a time, in a lifter with a training history, so the cause of any change can be seen.\" |\n\nCounts: **GO 7, FIX 3, NO-GO 0.**\n\n## Confidence rule across the 10\n\nRule: established, parameterised practice the authors present with loads, reps, rest or other parameters is `supported`; anecdotes, novelties the authors discount, tip lists and unevidenced orderings are `untested`; `contested` only for a recorded same-question conflict, with the reason scoped.\n\n| Entry | Rating | Consistent? |\n|---|---|---|\n| Chart (Figs 7.1, 7.2) | untested | Yes. Taxonomy; the nervous-system arrow is an unevidenced ordering. |\n| Maximal / supramaximal / circa / submax | contested | Yes. Scoped to the rest between singles. |\n| Three bodybuilding regimes | supported | Yes. Sets, reps, load and rest; \"one of the best known\" and \"popularly used\" (PDF 408). The When NOT quote must not be the \"minimal significance\" frame that keeps the miscellaneous entry `untested` (FIX above). |\n| Set structures | supported | Yes. Parameterised; \"commonly used\" (PDF 408); pyramid values from Fig 1.9 (PDF 409). |\n| Interval and cluster | contested | Yes on the conflict; add the scope sentence (FIX above) so the interval prescriptions are not read as disputed. |\n| Hybrid | untested | Yes. Repetition counts only; the coach's own inventions (PDF 411). |\n| Segmental | untested | Yes, borderline (see row). |\n| Contrast | contested | Yes. Scoped to heavy-light. |\n| Miscellaneous | untested | Yes. Novelties of \"minimal significance\" (PDF 412). |\n| Sticking points | untested | Yes. Tip list, \"Among some of the methods are the following\" (PDF 413). |\n\nThe two upgrades to `supported` are justified by the page. The eight unchanged ratings are consistent with the rule.\n\n## Mechanical checks (all 10 files)\n\n- Six section headers in the vault form `**X** —`: 6 in every file.\n- Em dashes outside the six headers: none.\n- Wrapped wikilinks: none.\n- Review-history or process wording (round, reviewer, fixer, corrected, prior version, NO-GO, \"now reads\"): none.\n- Wikilinks: all 45 distinct targets resolve, in the staging library or the vault (`/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library`), including the two path-qualified Zatsiorsky links.\n\n## Missing\n\nNone new. The five round-1 missing ideas are placed in the chart entry and quoted correctly (PDF 404, 405, 414). The decision not to write a separate entry for low-intensity cyclical work (two sentences, no parameters) is reasonable.\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** All seven round-1 fixes landed and are correct on the page; the fixer's three disagreements with round 1 are justified. Three minor fixes remain:\n\n1. **Three regimes:** replace the \"minimal significance\" opening of When NOT (that PDF 412 sentence belongs to the Miscellaneous Methods frame) with the PDF 405 deficit-based limit given above.\n2. **Interval and cluster:** \"take\" to \"can take\" for the 4-6RM sentence, and add the scope sentence to \"Why `contested`\".\n3. **Sticking points:** label the \"for weeks\", \"training history\" and \"one or two changes at a time\" framing in When to apply as Inference.\n\nThese are wording edits with no source re-check needed; after they are applied the batch can be promoted without another review round.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b6c4c818-3030-4dde-a1f1-9583bc00cacf","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
